var LESSONS = [
  /* ===================================================================== */
  {
    id: 'dhcp',
    group: 'addr',
    title: 'DHCP',
    subtitle: 'Hands out IP addresses automatically',
    chip: 'UDP 67/68',
    ports: { s: 'Server listens on 67, clients on 68 (UDP).', m: 'UDP 67 (server) and UDP 68 (client). Broadcast on the local network.', e: 'UDP 67 server / 68 client (RFC 2131). Discover and Request are broadcast to 255.255.255.255; Offer and ACK are unicast to the client MAC or broadcast if the client asks. Relays (option 82, giaddr) carry it across subnets.' },
    winRole: 'DHCP Server',
    linuxPkgs: 'Ubuntu: kea-dhcp4-server (or isc-dhcp-server). Alma/Rocky: dhcp-server',
    answers: { s: '"What address should I use?"', m: 'An IP address, mask, gateway and DNS server for a client that has none', e: 'Lease of an IPv4 address plus options (1, 3, 6, 15, 51, 54) to a client identified by MAC' },
    oneLiner: {
      s: 'DHCP is the server that gives every new computer on the network an address, so nobody has to type one in.',
      m: 'DHCP leases each client an IP address, subnet mask, default gateway and DNS server automatically, for a fixed time, from a pool the administrator defines.',
      e: 'DHCP (RFC 2131) leases IPv4 configuration from a scope to clients identified by MAC or client-id, via a four-message broadcast exchange, and clients renew unicast at half the lease time.'
    },
    project: {
      s: 'Your PC needs an address before it can ask DNS anything or load a page. DHCP gives it that address and also tells it where the DNS server is.',
      m: 'The client in the project gets its address, gateway and, above all, its DNS server (option 6) from this server. If option 6 points at the wrong place, the name lookup in the next step fails even though DHCP "worked".',
      e: 'Scope options 3 (router) and 6 (DNS) must point at the lab gateway and the DNS server holding the <code>lab.local</code> zone. Option 15 (domain name) lets clients type <code>www</code> instead of <code>www.lab.local</code>. Reserve or exclude the web server\'s address.'
    },
    sections: [
      { h: 'What it does', p: [
        { s: 'Every computer on a network needs a unique address, like every house on a street needs a number. You could walk around and paint a number on each one by hand. DHCP is the office that hands out numbers automatically when a computer shows up, and takes them back when it leaves.',
          m: 'Every host needs a unique IP address, a subnet mask, a default gateway and a DNS server before it can do anything useful. Typing those into hundreds of machines is slow and error-prone, so a <b>DHCP server</b> hands them out on request. The set of addresses it may give out is a <b>scope</b> (Windows) or <b>subnet/pool</b> (Linux).',
          e: 'DHCP centralises IPv4 configuration: a scope is a subnet with a pool range, exclusions, a lease duration and options. Clients receive an address plus options 1 (mask), 3 (router), 6 (DNS), 15 (domain), 51 (lease time), 54 (server identifier). Reservations bind a MAC to a fixed address; exclusions keep manually assigned addresses out of the pool.' },
        { s: 'The address is a <b>lease</b>: it is yours for a while (often a day), then you have to ask again. That way addresses come back when laptops leave.',
          m: 'The address is a <b>lease</b> with an expiry. Halfway through the lease the client quietly asks the same server to renew. If it never renews, the address returns to the pool. Servers, printers and the gateway should <b>not</b> use pool addresses: give them a <b>reservation</b> (always the same address, by MAC) or a static address outside the pool.',
          e: 'Lease timers: T1 = 50% (unicast Request to the leasing server), T2 = 87.5% (broadcast Request to any server), expiry (address released, client restarts with Discover). Typical LAN lease 8 h (Windows default) to 24 h; short leases on guest networks. Reservations are per-MAC (or client-id) and can carry their own options.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'How it works: the DORA exchange', diagram: true, p: [
        { s: 'A computer that just booted has no address, so it cannot talk to anyone directly. Instead it shouts to the whole room, and the DHCP server shouts back. Four messages: <b>D</b>iscover, <b>O</b>ffer, <b>R</b>equest, <b>A</b>cknowledge. DORA.',
          m: 'The client has no IP address yet, so it <b>broadcasts</b>. Four messages, remembered as <b>DORA</b>: the client <b>Discovers</b>, the server <b>Offers</b> an address, the client <b>Requests</b> that address (so if two servers offered, everyone hears which one won), and the server <b>Acknowledges</b> with the lease and all the options.',
          e: 'DORA: Discover (broadcast, source 0.0.0.0:68 to 255.255.255.255:67, client MAC in chaddr, option 53=1), Offer (yiaddr proposed, option 53=2), Request (broadcast, option 50 requested address, option 54 chosen server, so other servers withdraw), ACK (option 53=5, lease and options). NAK if the request is invalid. Transaction id (xid) ties the four together.' }
      ], after: [
        { s: 'Later, when half the lease has passed, the computer asks "can I keep it?" directly to the server it got it from. That is the dashed line.',
          m: 'The dashed step is the <b>renewal</b> at half the lease time: it is a plain unicast Request/ACK, no broadcast, because the client now knows the server\'s address.',
          e: 'At T1 the client unicasts Request to the server identifier (option 54); ACK extends the lease. At T2 it broadcasts. On link change it sends Request for its old address first (INIT-REBOOT) and only falls back to Discover on NAK or timeout.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'Set it up', p: [
        { s: 'Give the DHCP server itself a fixed address first. A server that hands out addresses cannot be waiting for one.',
          m: 'The DHCP server must have a <b>static</b> IP address on the network it serves. Decide the pool (for example .100 to .200), what to exclude (servers, printers, the gateway) and the options every client should receive: gateway (option 3), DNS server (option 6), domain name (option 15).',
          e: 'Prerequisites: static address on the served interface; the pool must not overlap static assignments; options 3, 6, 15 set at scope level; lease 8 to 24 h. On a routed network the router needs a DHCP relay (Cisco <code>ip helper-address</code>) pointing at this server, and the server needs a scope for each remote subnet, matched by giaddr.' }
      ], os: {
        win: {
          intro: { s: 'Windows Server does this with a wizard. You add the DHCP role, then create a scope and tell it what to hand out.', m: 'Server Manager adds the role; the DHCP console (dhcpmgmt.msc) creates the scope. In a domain the server must also be <b>authorized</b> in Active Directory before it will answer.', e: 'Role install, AD authorization (a rogue-server control: unauthorized Windows DHCP servers in an AD domain stop serving), then scope, exclusions, options and activation. Every step has a PowerShell equivalent in the DhcpServer module.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Set a fixed IP address on the server (Settings > Network > Change adapter options > IPv4 properties).', m: 'Give the server a static IPv4 address, mask, gateway and DNS server, for example 192.168.110.10/24.', e: 'Static addressing:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'New-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -IPAddress 192.168.110.10 -PrefixLength 24 -DefaultGateway 192.168.110.1\nSet-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -ServerAddresses 192.168.110.10' } },
            { text: { s: 'Open <b>Server Manager</b>, choose <b>Add roles and features</b>, tick <b>DHCP Server</b>, and click through to Install.', m: 'Server Manager > Manage > <b>Add Roles and Features</b> > Role-based > this server > tick <b>DHCP Server</b> > add the management tools > Install.', e: 'Install the role and tools:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Install-WindowsFeature DHCP -IncludeManagementTools' } },
            { text: { s: 'After the install, click the yellow flag in Server Manager and finish the <b>DHCP post-install</b> step. In a domain this "authorizes" the server so the network trusts it.', m: 'Click the notification flag > <b>Complete DHCP configuration</b>. This creates the two local groups (DHCP Administrators, DHCP Users) and, in a domain, <b>authorizes</b> the server in AD. A stand-alone (workgroup) server skips authorization.', e: 'Post-install: security groups and AD authorization.' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DhcpServerInDC -DnsName dc1.lab.local -IPAddress 192.168.110.10\nRestart-Service DHCPServer' } },
            { text: { s: 'Open the <b>DHCP</b> console, right-click <b>IPv4</b> and choose <b>New Scope</b>. Name it, give it the first and last address to hand out (for example 192.168.110.100 to .200) and the mask.', m: 'Tools > <b>DHCP</b> > expand the server > right-click <b>IPv4</b> > <b>New Scope</b>. Name: Lab. Range 192.168.110.100 to 192.168.110.200, length 24, mask 255.255.255.0.', e: 'Create the scope:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DhcpServerv4Scope -Name "Lab" -StartRange 192.168.110.100 -EndRange 192.168.110.200 -SubnetMask 255.255.255.0 -LeaseDuration 1.00:00:00 -State Active' } },
            { text: { s: 'The wizard asks for <b>exclusions</b> (addresses inside the range you never want given out), how long a <b>lease</b> lasts, then the <b>router</b>, <b>DNS server</b> and <b>domain name</b> to hand out. Type the gateway address and the DNS server address here.', m: 'Exclusions: none needed if servers sit outside the range. Lease: 1 day is fine for a lab. <b>Configure options now</b>: Router 192.168.110.1, Parent domain lab.local, DNS server 192.168.110.10. Activate the scope at the end.', e: 'Scope options 3, 6, 15:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Set-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId 192.168.110.0 -Router 192.168.110.1 -DnsServer 192.168.110.10 -DnsDomain lab.local' } },
            { text: { s: 'Give the web server a <b>reservation</b>: right-click Reservations > New Reservation, type its name, the address it should always get, and its MAC address.', m: 'Reservation for the web server so its address never changes: Reservations > <b>New Reservation</b>: name web1, IP 192.168.110.20, MAC from <code>ipconfig /all</code> on that machine (Physical Address, without dashes is fine).', e: 'Reservation:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DhcpServerv4Reservation -ScopeId 192.168.110.0 -IPAddress 192.168.110.20 -ClientId "00-15-5D-01-02-03" -Name web1' } }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Green icon on the scope means it is active and handing out addresses.', m: 'A green tick on the IPv4 node means the server is authorized and the scope is active. Address Leases shows every client as it arrives.', e: '<code>Get-DhcpServerv4Scope</code>, <code>Get-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 192.168.110.0</code>, <code>Get-DhcpServerv4Statistics</code>. Windows also runs DHCP audit logs in <code>%SystemRoot%\\System32\\dhcp</code>.' }
        },
        ubuntu: {
          intro: { s: 'Ubuntu uses a program called Kea. You install it, write a short settings file, and start it.', m: 'Ubuntu 24.04 ships <b>Kea</b> (<code>kea-dhcp4-server</code>), the successor to the old ISC dhcpd. Its configuration is one JSON file.', e: 'Kea DHCPv4 (ISC). Config: <code>/etc/kea/kea-dhcp4.conf</code>, JSON. Lease database defaults to a memfile CSV in <code>/var/lib/kea</code>. The legacy <code>isc-dhcp-server</code> is still packaged but end-of-life.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Give the server a fixed address. Ubuntu Server uses a file in /etc/netplan for this.', m: 'Static address with netplan (the file name varies; <code>ls /etc/netplan</code>). Then apply it.', e: 'Netplan static configuration:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/netplan/50-cloud-init.yaml', out: 'network:\n  version: 2\n  ethernets:\n    ens18:\n      addresses: [192.168.110.10/24]\n      routes:\n        - to: default\n          via: 192.168.110.1\n      nameservers:\n        addresses: [192.168.110.10]' },
            { text: '', cmd: 'sudo netplan apply\nip -br addr', out: 'ens18    UP    192.168.110.10/24' },
            { text: { s: 'Install Kea.', m: 'Install the DHCPv4 server package.', e: 'Install:' }, cmd: 'sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y kea-dhcp4-server' },
            { text: { s: 'Replace the settings file with this one. It names the network card, the pool of addresses, and the gateway and DNS server to hand out.', m: 'Edit <code>/etc/kea/kea-dhcp4.conf</code>. The important parts: which interface to listen on, the <code>subnet4</code> with its <code>pools</code>, the lease time, and the <code>option-data</code> (routers = option 3, domain-name-servers = option 6, domain-name = option 15).', e: 'Minimal configuration (memfile lease DB, one subnet, a reservation for the web server):' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/kea/kea-dhcp4.conf', out: '{ "Dhcp4": {\n  "interfaces-config": { "interfaces": [ "ens18" ] },\n  "lease-database": { "type": "memfile", "persist": true },\n  "valid-lifetime": 86400,\n  "option-data": [\n    { "name": "domain-name-servers", "data": "192.168.110.10" },\n    { "name": "domain-name", "data": "lab.local" }\n  ],\n  "subnet4": [ {\n    "id": 1,\n    "subnet": "192.168.110.0/24",\n    "pools": [ { "pool": "192.168.110.100 - 192.168.110.200" } ],\n    "option-data": [ { "name": "routers", "data": "192.168.110.1" } ],\n    "reservations": [ { "hw-address": "00:15:5d:01:02:03", "ip-address": "192.168.110.20", "hostname": "web1" } ]\n  } ]\n} }' },
            { text: { s: 'Check the file for typos, then start the server and make it start at boot.', m: 'Test the configuration, then enable and start the service.', e: 'Syntax check, enable, verify:' }, cmd: 'sudo kea-dhcp4 -t /etc/kea/kea-dhcp4.conf\nsudo systemctl enable --now kea-dhcp4-server\nsystemctl status kea-dhcp4-server --no-pager', out: '● kea-dhcp4-server.service - Kea IPv4 DHCP daemon\n     Active: active (running)' },
            { text: { s: 'If the firewall is on, let DHCP through.', m: 'ufw is off by default on Ubuntu; if you enabled it, allow UDP 67.', e: 'Firewall:' }, cmd: 'sudo ufw allow 67/udp' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Watch addresses being handed out with the log command below.', m: 'Leases appear in <code>/var/lib/kea/kea-leases4.csv</code>; live activity is in the journal: <code>journalctl -u kea-dhcp4-server -f</code>.', e: '<code>journalctl -u kea-dhcp4-server -f</code> shows DHCP4_LEASE_ALLOC lines with xid, MAC and address. Only one DHCP server per broadcast domain: make sure the lab router\'s own DHCP is off.' }
        },
        rhel: {
          intro: { s: 'Alma and Rocky use the classic ISC DHCP server. One settings file, then start it.', m: 'RHEL-family systems package ISC <b>dhcpd</b> as <code>dhcp-server</code>. Configuration is <code>/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf</code>. The firewall (firewalld) is on by default and must allow DHCP.', e: 'ISC dhcpd 4.4 (<code>dhcp-server</code>). Config <code>/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf</code>; leases in <code>/var/lib/dhcpd/dhcpd.leases</code>. firewalld has a predefined <code>dhcp</code> service. SELinux permits dhcpd on UDP 67 by default.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Give the server a fixed address with nmcli (or nmtui, which is a menu).', m: 'Static address with NetworkManager. Find the connection name with <code>nmcli con show</code>.', e: 'Static addressing:' }, cmd: 'sudo nmcli con mod "ens18" ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 192.168.110.10/24 ipv4.gateway 192.168.110.1 ipv4.dns 192.168.110.10\nsudo nmcli con up "ens18"\nip -br addr', out: 'ens18    UP    192.168.110.10/24' },
            { text: { s: 'Install the DHCP server.', m: 'Install the package.', e: 'Install:' }, cmd: 'sudo dnf install -y dhcp-server' },
            { text: { s: 'Write the settings file. It says which network to serve, the range of addresses to give out, and the gateway and DNS server to hand out.', m: 'Edit <code>/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf</code>. The <code>subnet</code> block must match a network the server has an address on, or dhcpd refuses to start.', e: 'Configuration (authoritative, one subnet, a host reservation):' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf', out: 'authoritative;\ndefault-lease-time 86400;\nmax-lease-time 172800;\noption domain-name "lab.local";\noption domain-name-servers 192.168.110.10;\n\nsubnet 192.168.110.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {\n  range 192.168.110.100 192.168.110.200;\n  option routers 192.168.110.1;\n  option broadcast-address 192.168.110.255;\n}\n\nhost web1 {\n  hardware ethernet 00:15:5d:01:02:03;\n  fixed-address 192.168.110.20;\n}' },
            { text: { s: 'Check for typos, then start it and make it start at boot.', m: 'Test the file, then enable and start the service.', e: 'Syntax check, enable, verify:' }, cmd: 'sudo dhcpd -t -cf /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf\nsudo systemctl enable --now dhcpd\nsystemctl status dhcpd --no-pager', out: '● dhcpd.service - DHCPv4 Server Daemon\n     Active: active (running)' },
            { text: { s: 'Open the firewall for DHCP. This step is easy to forget and is the usual reason nothing gets an address.', m: 'firewalld is on: allow the dhcp service permanently and reload.', e: 'Firewall:' }, cmd: 'sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dhcp\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\nsudo firewall-cmd --list-services', out: 'cockpit dhcp dhcpv6-client ssh' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'The log command below shows each address as it is handed out.', m: 'Leases are in <code>/var/lib/dhcpd/dhcpd.leases</code>; live activity: <code>journalctl -u dhcpd -f</code>.', e: '<code>journalctl -u dhcpd -f</code> shows DHCPDISCOVER / DHCPOFFER / DHCPREQUEST / DHCPACK lines with MAC and address, the literal DORA in the log.' }
        }
      }}
    ],
    actors: [{ name: 'Client PC', addr: '0.0.0.0 then .150' }, { name: 'DHCP server', addr: '192.168.110.10' }],
    steps: [
      { from: 0, to: 'all', label: { s: 'Discover: "Anyone have an address for me?"', m: 'Discover  (broadcast, from 0.0.0.0)', e: 'DHCPDISCOVER  0.0.0.0:68 > 255.255.255.255:67, xid, chaddr' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: 'Offer: "How about 192.168.110.150?"', m: 'Offer  192.168.110.150, mask, router, DNS, lease', e: 'DHCPOFFER  yiaddr .150, opt 1/3/6/51/54' } },
      { from: 0, to: 'all', label: { s: 'Request: "Yes please, .150 from you"', m: 'Request  .150 from server .10  (broadcast)', e: 'DHCPREQUEST  opt 50 = .150, opt 54 = .10  (broadcast)' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: 'Acknowledge: "It is yours for 24 hours"', m: 'ACK  lease 86400 s + all options', e: 'DHCPACK  opt 51 = 86400, 1/3/6/15/54' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, dashed: true, label: { s: 'Later: "Can I keep it?"  "Yes."', m: 'Renew at half the lease  (unicast Request / ACK)', e: 'T1 = 43200 s: unicast DHCPREQUEST to opt 54, DHCPACK' } }
    ],
    checkIntro: [
      { s: 'On a second computer, set its network card to "get an address automatically" and plug it into the same network. Then look at what it got.',
        m: 'On a client on the same network, with the adapter set to obtain an address automatically, release and renew, then read the result. You are checking four things: the address is in your pool, the mask is right, the gateway is option 3, the DNS server is option 6.',
        e: 'Verify from a client: address within the pool (or the reservation), correct mask, options 3, 6, 15 and 51 as configured, and the DHCP server field naming your server (option 54). Then confirm the lease on the server side.' }
    ],
    check: [
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'ipconfig /release\nipconfig /renew\nipconfig /all', out: 'Ethernet adapter Ethernet:\n   DHCP Enabled. . . . . . . . . . . : Yes\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.110.150(Preferred)\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Lease Obtained. . . . . . . . . . : Monday, 14 September 2026 09:12:03\n   Lease Expires . . . . . . . . . . : Tuesday, 15 September 2026 09:12:03\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.110.1\n   DHCP Server . . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.110.10\n   DNS Servers . . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.110.10\n   Connection-specific DNS Suffix  . : lab.local',
        why: { s: 'The address should be inside your pool, and "DHCP Server" should be your server. A "169.254" address means nobody answered.', m: '<b>DHCP Server</b> must be your server\'s address (if a second server is on the network it shows here). <b>Lease Expires</b> minus <b>Lease Obtained</b> is your lease time. An address starting <b>169.254</b> is APIPA: the client gave up waiting for an Offer.', e: 'Confirms options 1, 3, 6, 15, 51 and 54. 169.254.0.0/16 = APIPA (RFC 3927) after four Discovers with no Offer. <code>ipconfig /all</code> also shows the DUID/IAID if IPv6 is on; ignore for this lab.' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'Get-DhcpServerv4Lease -ComputerName dc1 -ScopeId 192.168.110.0', out: 'IPAddress        ScopeId          ClientId              HostName   AddressState\n---------        -------          --------              --------   ------------\n192.168.110.150  192.168.110.0    08-00-27-4a-1b-2c     pc1        Active\n192.168.110.20   192.168.110.0    00-15-5d-01-02-03     web1       ActiveReservation',
        why: { s: 'The same lease seen from the server side.', m: 'Run on the server (or with RSAT). The lease for the client should be listed as Active, the web server as ActiveReservation.', e: 'Server-side view of the lease table; <code>Get-DhcpServerv4Statistics</code> shows pool utilisation, <code>Get-DhcpServerv4FreeIPAddress</code> the next free address.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'sudo dhclient -v -r ens18; sudo dhclient -v ens18', out: 'DHCPDISCOVER on ens18 to 255.255.255.255 port 67 interval 3\nDHCPOFFER of 192.168.110.151 from 192.168.110.10\nDHCPREQUEST for 192.168.110.151 on ens18 to 255.255.255.255 port 67\nDHCPACK of 192.168.110.151 from 192.168.110.10\nbound to 192.168.110.151 -- renewal in 39233 seconds.',
        why: { s: 'This prints the four DORA messages as they happen. If it stops after DISCOVER, the server never answered.', m: 'The four DORA lines in order, then the renewal time (a random value near half the lease). If you only see DHCPDISCOVER repeating, no server heard you: wrong network, server down, or firewall.', e: 'dhclient with -v traces the state machine. On NetworkManager systems use <code>nmcli dev show ens18 | grep -i dhcp4</code> to read the received options instead; on Ubuntu desktops/servers with systemd-networkd, <code>networkctl status ens18</code>.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'ip -4 addr show ens18; ip route; resolvectl status ens18 | grep "DNS Servers"', out: '    inet 192.168.110.151/24 brd 192.168.110.255 scope global dynamic ens18\n       valid_lft 86395sec preferred_lft 86395sec\ndefault via 192.168.110.1 dev ens18 proto dhcp\n192.168.110.0/24 dev ens18 proto kernel scope link src 192.168.110.151\n     DNS Servers: 192.168.110.10',
        why: { s: '"dynamic" means it came from DHCP. The remaining lifetime counts down to the lease end.', m: '<b>dynamic</b> marks a DHCP address; <b>valid_lft</b> is the remaining lease; the default route <b>proto dhcp</b> is option 3; the DNS server is option 6.', e: 'valid_lft is the kernel view of option 51 counting down. <code>proto dhcp</code> on the default route proves option 3 was applied. resolvectl shows the per-link resolver from option 6.' } }
    ],
    protocolRow: 'udp', protocolRowTitle: 'UDP (DHCP DORA)',
    breaks: [
      { s: '<b>Address starts with 169.254.</b> Nobody answered. Is the server running? Is the client on the same network? On Alma/Rocky, did you open the firewall?', m: '<b>APIPA (169.254.x.x).</b> No Offer arrived: service stopped, scope not active, server not authorized (Windows in a domain), firewall blocking UDP 67 (firewalld on Alma/Rocky), or the client is in a different VLAN with no relay.', e: '<b>169.254/16:</b> no DHCPOFFER. Check in order: service state; scope Active and authorized (<code>Get-DhcpServerInDC</code>); host firewall UDP 67; same broadcast domain or <code>ip helper-address</code> on the router; server-side log shows DISCOVER arriving or not, which splits network from server problems.' },
      { s: '<b>Got an address, but from the wrong place.</b> Another box (often the Wi-Fi router) is also handing out addresses. Turn its DHCP off.', m: '<b>Wrong DHCP Server address in ipconfig.</b> Two DHCP servers on one network: usually the lab router\'s built-in one. Disable it; clients take the first Offer they hear.', e: '<b>Rogue/second server:</b> clients accept the first Offer, so results look random. Windows DHCP in a domain refuses to serve if unauthorized, which is why authorization exists. Find the culprit by MAC of the Offer in a capture.' },
      { s: '<b>Address is fine, but web pages by name do not load.</b> The DNS server DHCP handed out is wrong. Fix option 6 and renew.', m: '<b>Wrong option 6 (DNS).</b> DHCP worked perfectly, but the client is asking the wrong DNS server. Fix the scope option, then <code>ipconfig /renew</code> on the client. This is the most common "DHCP" ticket that is not a DHCP problem.', e: '<b>Option 6 misconfigured</b> (or set at server level and overridden at scope level). Verify with <code>Get-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId</code>; clients need a renew to pick up changed options.' },
      { s: '<b>Pool is full.</b> Too many devices for the range you gave. Make the range bigger or the lease shorter.', m: '<b>Scope exhausted.</b> The pool ran out; Windows logs event 1020 and the console shows 100% in Statistics. Widen the range or shorten the lease.', e: '<b>Exhaustion:</b> event 1020/1063 on Windows, "no free leases" in dhcpd/Kea logs. Remedies: larger pool, shorter lease, or a superscope / second subnet with a relay.' },
      { s: '<b>Server and client disagree on the network.</b> A typo in the mask or range means the server refuses to start (Linux) or hands out addresses that cannot reach the gateway.', m: '<b>Mask or range mismatch.</b> dhcpd will not start if no interface matches the subnet block; Kea logs the interface it could not bind. On Windows a wrong mask hands out clients that cannot reach the router.', e: '<b>Subnet/interface mismatch:</b> dhcpd "No subnet declaration for ens18"; Kea DHCP4_OPEN_SOCKET_FAIL. Confirm the server\'s own address lies inside the declared subnet and the pool excludes it.' }
    ]
  },
  /* ===================================================================== */
  {
    id: 'dns',
    group: 'addr',
    title: 'DNS',
    subtitle: 'Turns names into addresses',
    chip: 'UDP/TCP 53',
    ports: { s: 'Port 53. Usually UDP, TCP for big answers.', m: 'UDP 53 for normal queries; TCP 53 for zone transfers and answers too big for UDP.', e: 'UDP 53 (queries, EDNS0 up to 1232 bytes typical); TCP 53 for truncated responses (TC bit), zone transfers (AXFR/IXFR) and increasingly for all traffic; DoT 853 / DoH 443 on the client side.' },
    winRole: 'DNS Server',
    linuxPkgs: 'Ubuntu: bind9. Alma/Rocky: bind, bind-utils',
    answers: { s: '"What is the address of this name?"', m: 'The IP address (or mail server, or text) that belongs to a name', e: 'Resource records for a name and type: A, AAAA, MX, PTR, TXT, CNAME, NS, SRV, SOA' },
    oneLiner: {
      s: 'DNS is the phone book of the network: you give it a name, it gives you back the number, the IP address.',
      m: 'DNS resolves names such as www.lab.local to addresses by asking a chain of servers, each responsible for one part of the name, and caches the answers.',
      e: 'DNS (RFC 1034/1035) is a distributed, hierarchical database of resource records; recursive resolvers walk from the root through TLD and authoritative servers and cache each answer for its TTL.'
    },
    project: {
      s: 'This is where you give your web server a name. You create one <b>A record</b>: "www is at 192.168.110.20". After that, everyone can type the name instead of the number.',
      m: 'You create the zone <code>lab.local</code> and inside it an <b>A record</b>: <code>www.lab.local</code> = 192.168.110.20. The client learned this server\'s address from DHCP option 6, so its browser will ask here. While you are in the zone, add the other record types too, so you can see how each one answers.',
      e: 'Authoritative zone <code>lab.local</code> (forward) and <code>110.168.192.in-addr.arpa</code> (reverse) on the server named in DHCP option 6. Records: A www, AAAA www (if you have IPv6), MX for the zone apex, PTR for the web server, a TXT record. Set forwarders so names outside the lab still resolve.'
    },
    sections: [
      { h: 'What it does', p: [
        { s: 'People remember names; computers need numbers. Every time you type a name into a browser, your computer first asks a DNS server "what is the address for this?" and only then connects. You never see it happen because it takes a few milliseconds.',
          m: 'Applications work with names; IP works with addresses. Before any connection, the client\'s <b>resolver</b> asks its configured DNS server for the record it needs. That server either knows (it is <b>authoritative</b> for the zone, or has the answer <b>cached</b>) or goes and finds out (<b>recursion</b>).',
          e: 'The stub resolver on the client sends a query (name, type, class IN) to the recursive server from DHCP option 6. The recursive server answers from cache or iterates: root hints, TLD referral, authoritative answer. Answers carry a TTL; negative answers are cached per the SOA minimum (RFC 2308).' },
        { s: 'A DNS server keeps its names in a <b>zone</b>, a list of records for one domain such as lab.local. Each record is a name, a type, and a value.',
          m: 'The data lives in <b>zones</b>. A zone is the set of records for one domain (lab.local) held on the servers authoritative for it. A <b>forward</b> zone maps names to addresses; a <b>reverse</b> zone (in-addr.arpa) maps addresses back to names. Records have a <b>TTL</b>: how long anyone may cache them.',
          e: 'Zone = one SOA record plus its subtree, served by the NS set. Forward zones (lab.local) hold A/AAAA/CNAME/MX/TXT/SRV; reverse zones (110.168.192.in-addr.arpa) hold PTR. Primary/secondary replication via AXFR/IXFR and NOTIFY, or via AD replication for AD-integrated zones. Serial in the SOA must increase for secondaries to pull changes.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'The record types you will create', p: [
        { s: 'A zone can hold several kinds of records. Here are the five you will make in this project and what each one answers.',
          m: 'Every record has a name, a type, a TTL and a value. These are the types you will create in the lab zone, with the question each one answers.',
          e: 'RR types (RFC 1035 unless noted) used in the lab. Each is queried with <code>nslookup -type=X</code> or <code>dig X</code>.' }
      ], table: {
        head: ['Type', 'Answers the question', 'Example in lab.local', 'Notes'],
        rows: [
          ['<b>A</b>', { s: '"What is the IPv4 address of this name?"', m: 'Name to IPv4 address', e: 'Name to a 32-bit IPv4 address (type 1)' }, '`www.lab.local  A  192.168.110.20', { s: 'The one that makes your web server reachable by name.', m: 'The project\'s key record. Several A records for one name = simple load spreading (round robin).', e: 'Multiple A RRs at one owner are returned as an RRset; resolvers rotate. This is the record the browser needs before the TCP SYN.' }],
          ['<b>AAAA</b>', { s: '"What is the IPv6 address?" (four A\'s because IPv6 addresses are four times longer)', m: 'Name to IPv6 address', e: 'Name to a 128-bit IPv6 address (type 28, RFC 3596)' }, '`www.lab.local  AAAA  2001:db8:110::20', { s: 'Only needed if your network uses IPv6.', m: 'Modern clients ask for AAAA and A at the same time and prefer IPv6 if both answer.', e: 'Dual-stack clients query A and AAAA in parallel; Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305) races the connections. A missing AAAA is a normal NODATA answer, not an error.' }],
          ['<b>MX</b>', { s: '"Which computer takes the mail for this domain?"', m: 'Mail exchanger for a domain', e: 'Mail exchanger: preference + host name (type 15)' }, '`lab.local  MX  10 mail.lab.local', { s: 'The value is a name, not an address, so that name needs an A record too. The number is a priority: lowest wins.', m: 'Points at a <b>host name</b> (which must have its own A record), never an IP. Lower preference is tried first; equal preferences share the load.', e: 'RDATA = preference (16-bit) + exchange (FQDN). MUST NOT point at a CNAME (RFC 2181). Sending MTAs sort by preference ascending. Owner is the zone apex for user@lab.local.' }],
          ['<b>PTR</b>', { s: '"What name belongs to this address?" The reverse of A.', m: 'IP address to name (reverse lookup)', e: 'Reverse mapping in in-addr.arpa / ip6.arpa (type 12)' }, '`20.110.168.192.in-addr.arpa  PTR  www.lab.local', { s: 'Lives in a separate, backwards-written zone. Useful for logs and for mail servers checking who is talking to them.', m: 'Lives in the <b>reverse zone</b>, with the address octets reversed. Mail servers, log tools and <code>nslookup 192.168.110.20</code> use it. Windows DNS can create the PTR automatically when you add the A record.', e: 'Octets reversed under in-addr.arpa; nibbles reversed under ip6.arpa. Forward-confirmed reverse DNS (PTR then A must agree) is a common anti-spam check. "Create associated PTR" in the Windows A-record dialog needs the reverse zone to exist first.' }],
          ['<b>TXT</b>', { s: '"Any notes attached to this name?" Free text, used to prove you own a domain and to say who may send mail as you.', m: 'Arbitrary text; today mostly SPF, DKIM, DMARC and domain-ownership proofs', e: 'One or more character-strings (type 16); used for SPF (RFC 7208), DKIM, DMARC, ACME/ownership challenges' }, '`lab.local  TXT  "v=spf1 mx -all"', { s: 'The example says "only my mail server may send mail for lab.local".', m: 'The SPF example means: only the hosts named in our MX record may send mail from @lab.local; reject everything else.', e: 'Strings up to 255 bytes each, concatenated by the reader. Multiple TXT RRs at one owner are allowed but SPF requires exactly one SPF-shaped record. Quote the value in zone files.' }]
        ]
      }, after: [
        { s: 'Two more you will bump into: <b>CNAME</b> is a nickname ("intranet is another name for www"), and <b>NS</b> names the servers that hold the zone.',
          m: 'You will also meet <b>CNAME</b> (an alias: <code>intranet.lab.local CNAME www.lab.local</code>; the client then looks up www), <b>NS</b> (which servers are authoritative for the zone), <b>SOA</b> (zone metadata: serial, refresh, TTLs) and, once you build AD DS, <b>SRV</b> (which host offers a service, on which port).',
          e: 'CNAME may not coexist with other RRs at the same owner and must not be at the apex. NS/SOA are created with the zone. SRV (RFC 2782) <code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local</code> is how domain members find a DC; AD registers these dynamically, which is why AD needs dynamic updates enabled on its zone.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'How it works: one lookup', diagram: true, p: [
        { s: 'Inside your lab, the answer is one hop: your PC asks the lab DNS server, which owns the lab.local list, and it answers straight away. For a name on the internet the lab server has to ask around: first the "root" servers, which point it to the servers for .net, which point it to the servers for that company, which finally give the address. Then it remembers the answer.',
          m: 'For <code>www.lab.local</code> the lab DNS server is <b>authoritative</b> and answers from its own zone (steps 1 and 2). For an internet name it <b>recurses</b>: it asks a root server, gets referred to the .net servers, asks them, gets referred to the domain\'s own servers, asks them, gets the answer, caches it, and returns it. The client only ever sends one question and gets one answer. Next time, the cached copy answers (dashed step).',
          e: 'Steps 1 and 2: authoritative answer, AA bit set. Steps 3 to 8: iterative resolution for an external name (root hints, referral to .net TLD servers, referral to the zone\'s NS, final answer), or a single hop to a configured <b>forwarder</b> instead. Cached RRsets are served until TTL expiry (step 9, dashed). Windows DNS defaults to forwarders if set, else root hints.' }
      ], after: [
        { s: 'A DNS server can also be told to hand every question it cannot answer to another server (for example the ISP\'s). That is a <b>forwarder</b>, and it is what most lab servers do instead of asking the root.',
          m: 'Most small servers do not walk the tree themselves: they <b>forward</b> everything they are not authoritative for to one or two upstream resolvers (the ISP, or 1.1.1.1 / 8.8.8.8). Forwarders are simpler and faster on a small network. Without either forwarders or root hints, only lab.local resolves.',
          e: 'Forwarders (Windows: server properties > Forwarders; BIND: <code>forwarders {}</code> with <code>forward only;</code>) replace iteration. Conditional forwarders send one zone to a specific server, used between two labs or to an AD domain. With <code>forward only</code>, forwarder failure = total external outage; <code>forward first</code> falls back to root hints.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'Set it up', p: [
        { s: 'Plan the zone first: the domain name (lab.local), the web server\'s address, and which upstream server to send unknown names to.',
          m: 'Decide: the zone name (<code>lab.local</code>), the reverse zone (<code>110.168.192.in-addr.arpa</code>), the records (A www, AAAA www if applicable, MX, PTR, TXT) and the forwarders. The DNS server must point at <b>itself</b> as its own DNS server, and DHCP option 6 must point at it.',
          e: 'Zone plan: forward <code>lab.local</code>, reverse <code>110.168.192.in-addr.arpa</code>; records A/AAAA www, MX apex, PTR .20, TXT apex; forwarders to the upstream resolver; recursion allowed for the lab subnet only. Static address; the server\'s own resolver points at 127.0.0.1 or its own address. If AD DS will be installed on this box, let the AD wizard create the DNS role and make the zone AD-integrated instead.' }
      ], os: {
        win: {
          intro: { s: 'Add the DNS role, create a zone, then add records with a right-click. Windows can add the reverse (PTR) record for you when you add the A record.', m: 'Server Manager adds the role; the DNS console (dnsmgmt.msc) creates zones and records. If this server is or will be a domain controller, the AD DS wizard installs DNS for you and the zone becomes AD-integrated; the record steps are then identical.', e: 'Standalone primary zone shown; on a DC choose AD-integrated (stored in the directory partition, secure dynamic updates). Every console step has a DnsServer module equivalent.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Make sure the server has a fixed address and uses <b>itself</b> as its DNS server.', m: 'Static address; set the preferred DNS server to the server\'s own address (or 127.0.0.1).', e: 'Resolver pointing at itself:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -ServerAddresses 192.168.110.10' } },
            { text: { s: 'Server Manager > Add roles and features > tick <b>DNS Server</b> > Install.', m: 'Server Manager > Manage > Add Roles and Features > tick <b>DNS Server</b> (include management tools) > Install.', e: 'Install the role:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Install-WindowsFeature DNS -IncludeManagementTools' } },
            { text: { s: 'Tools > <b>DNS</b>. Right-click <b>Forward Lookup Zones</b> > <b>New Zone</b>. Choose Primary zone, name it <b>lab.local</b>, accept the file name, choose "Do not allow dynamic updates" for now, Finish.', m: 'DNS console > Forward Lookup Zones > <b>New Zone</b>: Primary, name <code>lab.local</code>, zone file <code>lab.local.dns</code>, dynamic updates: none (nonsecure updates are a risk; on a DC pick "secure only").', e: 'Primary forward zone:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone -Name lab.local -ZoneFile lab.local.dns -DynamicUpdate None\n# on a domain controller instead:\n# Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone -Name lab.local -ReplicationScope Domain -DynamicUpdate Secure' } },
            { text: { s: 'Now the backwards zone for addresses: right-click <b>Reverse Lookup Zones</b> > New Zone > Primary > IPv4 > Network ID <b>192.168.110</b> > Finish.', m: 'Reverse Lookup Zones > New Zone > Primary > IPv4 reverse > Network ID 192.168.110 (the console builds <code>110.168.192.in-addr.arpa</code>) > no dynamic updates.', e: 'Reverse zone:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone -NetworkId 192.168.110.0/24 -ZoneFile 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa.dns -DynamicUpdate None' } },
            { text: { s: 'Right-click <b>lab.local</b> > <b>New Host (A or AAAA)</b>. Name <b>www</b>, IP address <b>192.168.110.20</b>, tick <b>Create associated pointer (PTR) record</b>, Add Host. This one record is the whole point of the project.', m: 'lab.local > <b>New Host (A or AAAA)</b>: name <code>www</code>, IP 192.168.110.20, tick <b>Create associated PTR record</b> (works because the reverse zone exists). Add a second host with an IPv6 address if the lab has one: the same dialog creates an AAAA.', e: 'A + PTR (and AAAA):' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DnsServerResourceRecordA -ZoneName lab.local -Name www -IPv4Address 192.168.110.20 -CreatePtr\nAdd-DnsServerResourceRecordAAAA -ZoneName lab.local -Name www -IPv6Address 2001:db8:110::20' } },
            { text: { s: 'Right-click lab.local > <b>New Mail Exchanger (MX)</b>. Leave the host name blank (that means the domain itself), FQDN of mail server <b>mail.lab.local</b>, priority 10. Then add an A record for <b>mail</b> too, or the MX points at nothing.', m: 'lab.local > <b>New Mail Exchanger (MX)</b>: host blank (apex), mail server <code>mail.lab.local</code>, priority 10. An MX must name a host with an A record, so also New Host: <code>mail</code> = 192.168.110.25.', e: 'MX at the apex plus the A record it depends on:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DnsServerResourceRecordMX -ZoneName lab.local -Name "." -MailExchange mail.lab.local -Preference 10\nAdd-DnsServerResourceRecordA -ZoneName lab.local -Name mail -IPv4Address 192.168.110.25 -CreatePtr' } },
            { text: { s: 'Right-click lab.local > <b>Other New Records</b> > <b>Text (TXT)</b>. Leave the name blank, text: <b>v=spf1 mx -all</b>.', m: 'lab.local > Other New Records > <b>Text (TXT)</b>: record name blank (apex), text <code>v=spf1 mx -all</code>.', e: 'TXT (SPF) at the apex:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DnsServerResourceRecord -ZoneName lab.local -Name "." -Txt -DescriptiveText "v=spf1 mx -all"' } },
            { text: { s: 'Finally, tell the server where to send names it does not know: right-click the server name > Properties > <b>Forwarders</b> > Edit > type your upstream DNS server (the one your lab router or ISP uses).', m: 'Server > Properties > <b>Forwarders</b> > Edit: add the upstream resolver (e.g. 192.168.110.1 or 1.1.1.1). Without this, only lab.local resolves and the internet "breaks" for every client using this server.', e: 'Forwarders:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Set-DnsServerForwarder -IPAddress 1.1.1.1,9.9.9.9 -UseRootHint $false\nClear-DnsServerCache' } }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Click lab.local in the console: you should see the records you made, plus SOA and NS that Windows created.', m: 'The zone view shows SOA, NS (this server), A www, A mail, AAAA www, MX, TXT. The reverse zone shows the two PTRs. Right-click the zone > Reload if something looks stale.', e: '<code>Get-DnsServerResourceRecord -ZoneName lab.local</code> lists the RRset; <code>Get-DnsServerZone</code> confirms zone type. Event Viewer > Applications and Services Logs > DNS Server for load errors.' }
        },
        ubuntu: {
          intro: { s: 'Ubuntu uses BIND, the oldest and most common DNS server. You edit two kinds of files: the main settings, and one zone file per zone.', m: '<b>BIND 9</b> (<code>bind9</code>). Options in <code>/etc/bind/named.conf.options</code>, zone declarations in <code>/etc/bind/named.conf.local</code>, zone data in files you create. Ubuntu runs named as user <code>bind</code>.', e: 'BIND 9.18 on Ubuntu 24.04. Config split: named.conf.options (recursion, forwarders, listen-on), named.conf.local (zone stanzas), zone files under /etc/bind. Ubuntu\'s AppArmor profile allows writes only under /var/lib/bind and /var/cache/bind; keep static zones in /etc/bind.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Install BIND and its tools.', m: 'Install the server and the dig/nslookup utilities.', e: 'Install:' }, cmd: 'sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y bind9 bind9-utils bind9-dnsutils' },
            { text: { s: 'Edit the options file: who may ask questions (your lab network), and where to forward unknown names.', m: 'Edit <code>/etc/bind/named.conf.options</code>: allow recursion from the lab subnet only and set forwarders.', e: 'Options:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/bind/named.conf.options', out: 'acl lab { 192.168.110.0/24; 127.0.0.1; };\noptions {\n  directory "/var/cache/bind";\n  listen-on { any; };\n  listen-on-v6 { any; };\n  allow-query { any; };\n  allow-recursion { lab; };\n  recursion yes;\n  forwarders { 1.1.1.1; 9.9.9.9; };\n  forward only;\n  dnssec-validation auto;\n};' },
            { text: { s: 'Declare the two zones (names, and backwards addresses) and say which files hold them.', m: 'Edit <code>/etc/bind/named.conf.local</code>: one <code>zone</code> stanza for lab.local, one for the reverse zone.', e: 'Zone declarations:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/bind/named.conf.local', out: 'zone "lab.local" {\n  type primary;\n  file "/etc/bind/db.lab.local";\n};\nzone "110.168.192.in-addr.arpa" {\n  type primary;\n  file "/etc/bind/db.192.168.110";\n};' },
            { text: { s: 'Write the forward zone file. The first block (SOA) describes the zone; then come your records: A for www and mail, AAAA, MX, TXT.', m: 'Create <code>/etc/bind/db.lab.local</code>. The <b>serial</b> must go up every time you edit the file. Names without a trailing dot get the zone name appended; names with a dot are complete.', e: 'Forward zone (RFC 1035 master format; serial YYYYMMDDnn):' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/bind/db.lab.local', out: '$TTL 3600\n$ORIGIN lab.local.\n@       IN SOA  ns1.lab.local. admin.lab.local. (\n                2026091401 ; serial\n                3600       ; refresh\n                900        ; retry\n                1209600    ; expire\n                300 )      ; negative TTL\n@       IN NS   ns1.lab.local.\n@       IN MX   10 mail.lab.local.\n@       IN TXT  "v=spf1 mx -all"\nns1     IN A    192.168.110.10\nwww     IN A    192.168.110.20\nwww     IN AAAA 2001:db8:110::20\nmail    IN A    192.168.110.25\nintranet IN CNAME www.lab.local.' },
            { text: { s: 'Write the reverse zone file: the last part of each address, and the name it belongs to (PTR).', m: 'Create <code>/etc/bind/db.192.168.110</code> with a PTR per host. The owner is the last octet; $ORIGIN supplies the rest.', e: 'Reverse zone:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/bind/db.192.168.110', out: '$TTL 3600\n$ORIGIN 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa.\n@       IN SOA  ns1.lab.local. admin.lab.local. (\n                2026091401 3600 900 1209600 300 )\n@       IN NS   ns1.lab.local.\n10      IN PTR  ns1.lab.local.\n20      IN PTR  www.lab.local.\n25      IN PTR  mail.lab.local.' },
            { text: { s: 'Check the files for mistakes, then restart BIND.', m: 'Validate the config and both zones (the checker prints "OK" and the serial), then restart and enable.', e: 'Validate, restart:' }, cmd: 'sudo named-checkconf\nsudo named-checkzone lab.local /etc/bind/db.lab.local\nsudo named-checkzone 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa /etc/bind/db.192.168.110\nsudo systemctl restart named && sudo systemctl enable named', out: 'zone lab.local/IN: loaded serial 2026091401\nOK\nzone 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa/IN: loaded serial 2026091401\nOK' },
            { text: { s: 'Make the server use itself for DNS, and open the firewall if it is on.', m: 'Point the server\'s own resolver at itself (netplan <code>nameservers: addresses: [127.0.0.1]</code>, then <code>netplan apply</code>) and allow port 53 in ufw if enabled.', e: 'Own resolver and firewall:' }, cmd: 'sudo ufw allow 53\nresolvectl status | grep "DNS Servers"', out: '     DNS Servers: 127.0.0.1' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Test on the server itself before touching any client: <code>dig @127.0.0.1 www.lab.local</code>.', m: 'Test locally first: <code>dig @127.0.0.1 www.lab.local +short</code> should print 192.168.110.20. Then <code>journalctl -u named -f</code> while a client queries.', e: '<code>rndc status</code>, <code>rndc reload</code> after zone edits (with a bumped serial), <code>rndc querylog</code> to log queries, <code>rndc dumpdb -cache</code> to inspect the cache.' }
        },
        rhel: {
          intro: { s: 'Alma and Rocky also use BIND, but the files live in different places and the firewall is on by default.', m: '<b>BIND 9</b> from the <code>bind</code> package. Main config <code>/etc/named.conf</code>; zone files in <code>/var/named/</code>. firewalld must allow the <code>dns</code> service; SELinux expects zone files in /var/named with the right label (they get it automatically when created there).', e: 'BIND 9.16 on RHEL 9 family. Single /etc/named.conf (listens on localhost by default: change <code>listen-on</code>). Zone files in /var/named (context named_zone_t); dynamic zones belong in /var/named/dynamic. Use <code>named-chroot</code> if you want the chroot service instead.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Install BIND and its tools.', m: 'Install the server and utilities (dig, nslookup, host).', e: 'Install:' }, cmd: 'sudo dnf install -y bind bind-utils' },
            { text: { s: 'Edit the main file. By default it only answers itself; change it to listen on the network, allow your lab to ask, and forward the rest. Add the two zones at the bottom.', m: 'Edit <code>/etc/named.conf</code>: <code>listen-on</code> to any, <code>allow-query</code> to the lab, forwarders, and two zone stanzas at the end (before the include lines is fine).', e: 'named.conf (relevant parts):' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/named.conf', out: 'acl lab { 192.168.110.0/24; 127.0.0.1; };\noptions {\n  listen-on port 53 { any; };\n  listen-on-v6 port 53 { any; };\n  directory "/var/named";\n  allow-query { any; };\n  allow-recursion { lab; };\n  recursion yes;\n  forwarders { 1.1.1.1; 9.9.9.9; };\n  forward only;\n  dnssec-validation yes;\n};\nzone "lab.local" IN {\n  type primary;\n  file "lab.local.zone";\n};\nzone "110.168.192.in-addr.arpa" IN {\n  type primary;\n  file "192.168.110.rev";\n};' },
            { text: { s: 'Write the forward zone file in /var/named. Same records as on Ubuntu: SOA, NS, A, AAAA, MX, TXT.', m: 'Create <code>/var/named/lab.local.zone</code>. Bump the serial on every edit. Trailing dots make a name absolute.', e: 'Forward zone:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /var/named/lab.local.zone', out: '$TTL 3600\n$ORIGIN lab.local.\n@       IN SOA  ns1.lab.local. admin.lab.local. (\n                2026091401 3600 900 1209600 300 )\n@       IN NS   ns1.lab.local.\n@       IN MX   10 mail.lab.local.\n@       IN TXT  "v=spf1 mx -all"\nns1     IN A    192.168.110.10\nwww     IN A    192.168.110.20\nwww     IN AAAA 2001:db8:110::20\nmail    IN A    192.168.110.25\nintranet IN CNAME www.lab.local.' },
            { text: { s: 'Write the reverse zone file with the PTR records.', m: 'Create <code>/var/named/192.168.110.rev</code>.', e: 'Reverse zone:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /var/named/192.168.110.rev', out: '$TTL 3600\n$ORIGIN 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa.\n@       IN SOA  ns1.lab.local. admin.lab.local. (\n                2026091401 3600 900 1209600 300 )\n@       IN NS   ns1.lab.local.\n10      IN PTR  ns1.lab.local.\n20      IN PTR  www.lab.local.\n25      IN PTR  mail.lab.local.' },
            { text: { s: 'Let the named user read the files, check them, and start the service.', m: 'Fix ownership (files created by root are not readable by named), validate, then enable and start.', e: 'Permissions, validation, start:' }, cmd: 'sudo chown root:named /var/named/lab.local.zone /var/named/192.168.110.rev\nsudo chmod 640 /var/named/lab.local.zone /var/named/192.168.110.rev\nsudo named-checkconf\nsudo named-checkzone lab.local /var/named/lab.local.zone\nsudo systemctl enable --now named', out: 'zone lab.local/IN: loaded serial 2026091401\nOK' },
            { text: { s: 'Open the firewall for DNS and point the server at itself.', m: 'Allow the dns service in firewalld; set the server\'s own DNS to itself with nmcli.', e: 'Firewall and own resolver:' }, cmd: 'sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dns && sudo firewall-cmd --reload\nsudo nmcli con mod "ens18" ipv4.dns 127.0.0.1 && sudo nmcli con up "ens18"\ndig @127.0.0.1 www.lab.local +short', out: '192.168.110.20' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'If BIND refuses to start, the message is almost always in <code>journalctl -u named</code>: a typo, or a file it is not allowed to read.', m: 'Start failures: <code>journalctl -xeu named</code>. "permission denied" on a zone file is ownership or SELinux (<code>restorecon -Rv /var/named</code>).', e: 'SELinux: files must carry named_zone_t (<code>ls -Z /var/named</code>; <code>restorecon -Rv /var/named</code>). To allow named to write (dynamic updates), place the zone in /var/named/dynamic (named_cache_t). <code>rndc reload</code> after edits.' }
        }
      }}
    ],
    actors: [{ name: 'Client PC', addr: '192.168.110.150' }, { name: 'Lab DNS', addr: '192.168.110.10' }, { name: 'Root / .net / auth', addr: 'internet' }],
    steps: [
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Address of www.lab.local?"', m: 'Query  www.lab.local  A', e: 'Query  www.lab.local IN A  (RD=1)' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: '"192.168.110.20" (from its own list)', m: 'Answer  A 192.168.110.20  (authoritative)', e: 'Response  AA=1  A 192.168.110.20  TTL 3600' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Address of www.example.net?"', m: 'Query  www.example.net  A', e: 'Query  www.example.net IN A  (RD=1)' } },
      { from: 1, to: 2, label: { s: 'Root: "who handles .net?"', m: 'Query to a root server', e: 'Query  www.example.net A  to root (RD=0)' } },
      { from: 2, to: 1, label: { s: 'Root: "ask the .net servers"', m: 'Referral: .net NS records', e: 'Referral  net. NS a.gtld-servers.net + glue' } },
      { from: 1, to: 2, label: { s: '.net: "who handles example.net?"', m: 'Query to a .net server', e: 'Query  www.example.net A  to .net TLD' } },
      { from: 2, to: 1, label: { s: '.net: "ask example.net\'s own servers"', m: 'Referral: example.net NS records', e: 'Referral  example.net. NS ns1.example.net + glue' } },
      { from: 1, to: 2, label: { s: 'example.net: "address of www?"  "93.184.215.14"', m: 'Query to the authoritative server, answer', e: 'Query to ns1.example.net; Response AA=1  A 93.184.215.14' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: '"93.184.215.14" (and I will remember it)', m: 'Answer  A 93.184.215.14  (cached now)', e: 'Response  AA=0 RA=1  A 93.184.215.14  TTL as received' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, dashed: true, label: { s: 'Next time: answered from memory', m: 'Same query later: answered from cache', e: 'Repeat query within TTL: cache hit, TTL decremented' } }
    ],
    checkIntro: [
      { s: 'On a client that got its address from your DHCP server, look up each record you made. The answers should match what you typed into the zone.',
        m: 'From a DHCP client (so it is using your server via option 6), query each record type and compare with the zone. Then check that an internet name resolves too, which proves the forwarders work.',
        e: 'From a client using the server via option 6: verify authoritative answers (AA flag) for each RR type, the PTR from the reverse zone, and a recursive answer for an external name via the forwarders. Then confirm the client is actually using the server you think it is.' }
    ],
    check: [
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'nslookup www.lab.local', out: 'Server:  dc1.lab.local\nAddress:  192.168.110.10\n\nName:    www.lab.local\nAddress:  192.168.110.20',
        why: { s: 'The first two lines say which DNS server answered. The last line is your A record.', m: 'Lines 1 and 2 identify the server that answered (it must be yours). The Name/Address pair is the A record. If it says <b>Non-authoritative answer</b>, the client is using some other server that had to go and ask.', e: 'Server/Address come from a PTR lookup of the resolver itself, so a missing PTR for the DNS server shows "UnKnown" here (harmless but untidy). Absence of "Non-authoritative answer" confirms AA=1.' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'nslookup -type=MX lab.local\nnslookup -type=TXT lab.local\nnslookup -type=AAAA www.lab.local\nnslookup 192.168.110.20', out: 'lab.local       MX preference = 10, mail exchanger = mail.lab.local\nmail.lab.local  internet address = 192.168.110.25\n\nlab.local       text =\n        "v=spf1 mx -all"\n\nwww.lab.local   AAAA IPv6 address = 2001:db8:110::20\n\nName:    www.lab.local\nAddress:  192.168.110.20',
        why: { s: 'One command per record type. The last one asks backwards: address in, name out. That is the PTR.', m: 'Each <code>-type</code> asks for one record type. The plain address query at the end is the reverse lookup and only works because the PTR exists in the reverse zone. Note the MX answer also brings the A record of the mail host (an "additional" record) so the client does not have to ask again.', e: 'nslookup -type selects QTYPE. The MX response\'s additional section carries mail.lab.local A. The bare-IP query is a PTR query for 20.110.168.192.in-addr.arpa. <code>Resolve-DnsName www.lab.local -Type A -Server 192.168.110.10 | fl</code> gives the same with TTL and section.' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'nslookup www.google.com\nipconfig /displaydns | findstr /i "lab.local"', out: 'Server:  dc1.lab.local\nAddress:  192.168.110.10\n\nNon-authoritative answer:\nName:    www.google.com\nAddresses:  2607:f8b0:4004:c07::63\n          142.250.72.4\n\n    www.lab.local\n    Record Name . . . . . : www.lab.local',
        why: { s: 'An internet name should work too; if it does not, the "forwarders" step was missed. The second command shows what your PC has remembered.', m: 'The Google lookup proves forwarders (or root hints) work: "Non-authoritative" is correct here because your server is not authoritative for google.com. <code>ipconfig /displaydns</code> shows the client\'s own cache; <code>ipconfig /flushdns</code> empties it when you change a record and the client still sees the old value.', e: 'External resolution via forwarders: expect AA=0, RA=1. Client-side cache: Windows DNS Client service honours TTL; <code>Clear-DnsClientCache</code> to flush. Server-side: <code>Clear-DnsServerCache</code>.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'dig www.lab.local', out: ';; ->>HEADER<<- opcode: QUERY, status: NOERROR, id: 43121\n;; flags: qr aa rd ra; QUERY: 1, ANSWER: 1, AUTHORITY: 0, ADDITIONAL: 1\n\n;; ANSWER SECTION:\nwww.lab.local.          3600    IN      A       192.168.110.20\n\n;; Query time: 0 msec\n;; SERVER: 192.168.110.10#53(192.168.110.10) (UDP)',
        why: { s: 'The ANSWER SECTION is your record. SERVER at the bottom is who answered.', m: '<b>status: NOERROR</b> and one ANSWER. The <b>aa</b> flag means the server is authoritative for the zone. 3600 is the TTL you set. SERVER confirms which resolver the client used.', e: 'Flags: qr (response), aa (authoritative), rd/ra (recursion desired/available). NXDOMAIN = name does not exist; NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 = name exists but not that type (NODATA), e.g. asking AAAA where only A exists. SERVFAIL usually = zone failed to load or forwarders unreachable.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'dig MX lab.local +short\ndig TXT lab.local +short\ndig AAAA www.lab.local +short\ndig -x 192.168.110.20 +short\ndig @192.168.110.10 www.lab.local +norecurse +short', out: '10 mail.lab.local.\n"v=spf1 mx -all"\n2001:db8:110::20\nwww.lab.local.\n192.168.110.20',
        why: { s: 'One line per record type; <code>-x</code> is the reverse (PTR) lookup.', m: '<code>+short</code> prints just the values. <code>-x</code> builds the in-addr.arpa name for you. <code>@server</code> asks a specific server, which is how you test the DNS server before DHCP points anyone at it.', e: '<code>+norecurse</code> (RD=0) with <code>@server</code> proves the answer comes from the zone, not the cache or a forwarder. <code>dig +trace www.example.net</code> shows the full iteration from the root. <code>dig lab.local SOA</code> shows the serial secondaries will compare.' } }
    ],
    protocolRow: 'dns', protocolRowTitle: 'DNS',
    breaks: [
      { s: '<b>"Server not found" for www.lab.local.</b> First check which DNS server the client is using (ipconfig /all). If it is not yours, DHCP option 6 is wrong. If it is yours, the record is missing or misspelled.', m: '<b>Name does not resolve.</b> Split the problem: <code>nslookup www.lab.local 192.168.110.10</code> asks your server directly. Works? Then the client is using another server (fix DHCP option 6, renew). Fails? Then the zone or record is wrong: check spelling, and on Linux the serial and <code>named-checkzone</code>.', e: '<b>NXDOMAIN / SERVFAIL:</b> query the server explicitly with <code>@</code>. NXDOMAIN from your server = record missing or zone name mismatch (a trailing-dot mistake makes <code>www.lab.local.lab.local</code>). SERVFAIL = zone failed to load: <code>journalctl -u named</code> or DNS Server event log. Answer from a different server = option 6 or a static resolver on the client.' },
      { s: '<b>I changed the record but the client still sees the old address.</b> Both the client and the server remember answers for a while (the TTL). Run <code>ipconfig /flushdns</code> on the client and wait, or lower the TTL before you make changes.', m: '<b>Stale answer.</b> Caching, not a bug. The client cache (<code>ipconfig /flushdns</code>, <code>resolvectl flush-caches</code>) and the server cache (<code>Clear-DnsServerCache</code>, <code>rndc flush</code>) both hold the record for its TTL. Lower the TTL a day before a planned change.', e: '<b>TTL caching:</b> a changed RR propagates only after the old TTL expires everywhere it was cached, including browsers (Chrome keeps its own cache). On BIND, a forgotten serial bump means secondaries never pull the change at all.' },
      { s: '<b>Lab names work, internet names do not.</b> The server has no one to ask about names it does not own. Add forwarders.', m: '<b>External names fail.</b> No forwarders and no working root hints, or the forwarder is unreachable / blocked by the firewall. <code>nslookup www.google.com 1.1.1.1</code> from the server proves whether the server itself can reach the upstream.', e: '<b>Recursion failure:</b> SERVFAIL for external names only. Check forwarders (<code>Get-DnsServerForwarder</code>, named.conf forwarders), outbound UDP/TCP 53 from the server, and <code>allow-recursion</code> including the client subnet (BIND answers REFUSED otherwise).' },
      { s: '<b>Reverse lookup (address to name) fails.</b> The reverse zone or the PTR record is missing.', m: '<b>PTR missing.</b> The A record does not create a PTR unless you ticked the box (Windows) or wrote the reverse zone (Linux). Add the reverse zone first, then the PTR.', e: '<b>No PTR:</b> create the reverse zone before ticking "Create associated PTR"; existing A records can be fixed with <code>Add-DnsServerResourceRecordPtr</code>. Mail servers and some SSH configs care; browsing does not.' },
      { s: '<b>Linux: BIND will not start.</b> Almost always a typo in a config or zone file, or a file the named user cannot read. The check commands tell you exactly which line.', m: '<b>named fails to start.</b> Run <code>named-checkconf</code> and <code>named-checkzone</code>: they name the file and line. On Alma/Rocky also check ownership (root:named) and SELinux labels on files in /var/named.', e: '<b>Load errors:</b> missing trailing dot in SOA/NS names (creates ns1.lab.local.lab.local), CNAME at apex, MX pointing at a CNAME, duplicate SOA. RHEL: <code>ls -Z /var/named</code> should show named_zone_t; <code>ausearch -m avc -ts recent</code> for denials.' },
      { s: '<b>Windows: the DNS server itself cannot browse the internet.</b> Its own network card points at a DNS server that is not this one, or forwarders are missing.', m: '<b>Server\'s own resolver.</b> The DNS server must use itself (or 127.0.0.1) as its preferred DNS. A DC that points at the ISP\'s DNS breaks AD; a DNS server that points elsewhere gives confusing test results.', e: '<b>Resolver loop or bypass:</b> DC/DNS servers point at themselves (and a partner DC second). Check <code>Get-DnsClientServerAddress</code>; dcdiag /test:dns flags this on DCs.' }
    ]
  },
  /* ===================================================================== */
  {
    id: 'web',
    group: 'app',
    title: 'Web Server',
    subtitle: 'Answers HTTP requests with pages',
    chip: 'TCP 80/443',
    ports: { s: 'Port 80 for plain web pages, 443 for the padlock (HTTPS).', m: 'TCP 80 (HTTP) and TCP 443 (HTTPS). Other ports work but the browser must be told.', e: 'TCP 80 HTTP/1.1 (RFC 9112); TCP 443 HTTPS = HTTP over TLS, also HTTP/2; HTTP/3 uses UDP 443 (QUIC). A non-default port appears in the URL: http://host:8080/.' },
    winRole: 'Web Server (IIS)',
    linuxPkgs: 'Ubuntu: nginx (or apache2). Alma/Rocky: httpd (Apache) or nginx',
    answers: { s: '"Send me this page"', m: 'The requested file (HTML, image, script) plus a status code', e: 'An HTTP response: status line, headers, body, for each request on a persistent TCP connection' },
    oneLiner: {
      s: 'A web server is a program that waits for a browser to ask for a page and sends the page back.',
      m: 'A web server listens on TCP port 80 (and 443), receives HTTP requests for URLs, and answers each with a status code, headers and the file or generated content.',
      e: 'An HTTP server accepts TCP connections on 80/443, parses request lines and headers, maps the Host header and path to a site and a resource, and returns status, headers and body; TLS on 443 wraps the same exchange.'
    },
    project: {
      s: 'This is the thing your friend actually sees. You install a web server on the machine whose address you put in the DNS A record, and drop your HTML page into its folder.',
      m: 'Install the web server on the host the A record points at (192.168.110.20), put an <code>index.html</code> in its document root, open port 80 in the host firewall. Then <code>http://www.lab.local/</code> from a DHCP client exercises the whole chain: DHCP gave the client its DNS server, DNS gave it this address, and this server returns the page.',
      e: 'Host 192.168.110.20 (DHCP reservation or static), TCP 80 open in the host firewall, document root with index.html, optionally a second site selected by Host header to show name-based virtual hosting. HTTPS (self-signed or an internal CA) is the follow-on; the Encryption and Protocols site covers TLS.'
    },
    sections: [
      { h: 'What it does', p: [
        { s: 'When you visit a website, your browser sends a short text message to the server: "GET me the page called /". The server sends back "200 OK, here it is" followed by the page. Every image and script on the page is one more request and one more answer.',
          m: 'HTTP is a text protocol of <b>requests</b> and <b>responses</b>. The browser opens a TCP connection to port 80 and sends a request line (<code>GET / HTTP/1.1</code>), a <code>Host</code> header naming the site, and other headers. The server replies with a status line (<code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>), headers (<code>Content-Type</code>, <code>Content-Length</code>), a blank line, and the body: the HTML. Each image, stylesheet and script on the page is a further request, usually on the same connection.',
          e: 'HTTP/1.1: request = method, target, version; headers; optional body. Response = version, status, reason; headers; body. Persistent connections by default; Host header mandatory (name-based virtual hosting). Methods GET/HEAD/POST/PUT/DELETE; status classes 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error. HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over one TLS connection; HTTP/3 moves to QUIC.' },
        { s: 'The server keeps the pages in one folder, the <b>document root</b>. The address in the browser after the name is the path inside that folder: <code>/pictures/cat.jpg</code> is a file called cat.jpg in a folder called pictures. If the path is just <code>/</code>, the server sends <code>index.html</code>.',
          m: 'Static files come from the <b>document root</b> (IIS: <code>C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot</code>, nginx and httpd: <code>/var/www/html</code>). The URL path maps onto that folder; a bare <code>/</code> returns the <b>default document</b>, index.html. One server can host several <b>sites</b>, told apart by the <b>Host</b> header (name-based) or by address/port: IIS calls that a <b>binding</b>, nginx a <b>server block</b>, Apache a <b>virtual host</b>.',
          e: 'Document root per site; path normalisation prevents <code>..</code> escapes. Directory request without index: 403 (IIS, httpd default) or autoindex. Virtual hosting: IIS bindings (IP:port:hostname), nginx <code>server_name</code>, httpd <code>&lt;VirtualHost&gt;</code> + <code>ServerName</code>. Dynamic content via reverse proxy, FastCGI/PHP-FPM, ASP.NET modules; static serving is what the lab needs.' },
        { s: 'The status code is how the server says what happened: <b>200</b> fine, <b>301</b> moved, <b>403</b> not allowed, <b>404</b> no such page, <b>500</b> the server broke.',
          m: 'Status codes to know: <b>200</b> OK, <b>301/302</b> redirect (Location header), <b>304</b> not modified (cache), <b>403</b> forbidden, <b>404</b> not found, <b>500</b> server error, <b>502/503</b> the back end is down. The browser\'s developer tools (F12 > Network) show every request and its code.',
          e: '200/204; 301 permanent vs 302/307 temporary; 304 with If-Modified-Since/ETag; 400 malformed; 401 needs auth (WWW-Authenticate); 403; 404; 405; 413; 500; 502 upstream failure; 503 overload/maintenance. Log formats (W3C for IIS, combined for nginx/httpd) record method, path, status, bytes, referer, user agent.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'How it works: one page load', diagram: true, p: [
        { s: 'Before the page can move, three things happen in order: the name is looked up (DNS), a connection is opened (TCP, three tiny messages), and only then does the browser ask for the page. The whole thing takes a few hundredths of a second.',
          m: 'Order of events: DNS lookup for the name (step 1), TCP three-way handshake to port 80 (step 2, collapsed here), the HTTP request (step 3), the response with the HTML (step 4), then the browser parses the HTML and requests each referenced file on the same connection (steps 5 and 6).',
          e: 'Resolution (A/AAAA), TCP handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), request GET / with Host, response 200 with Content-Type text/html and Content-Length, then sub-resource requests reusing the persistent connection (Connection: keep-alive default in 1.1). With HTTPS a TLS handshake sits between steps 2 and 3.' }
      ], after: [
        { s: 'Notice that the web server never sees the name lookup. It only learns which site you wanted from the <b>Host</b> line inside the request. That is how one server can hold many websites.',
          m: 'DNS and HTTP never talk to each other. The server learns the requested site only from the <b>Host</b> header, which is why editing a client\'s hosts file to point a name at any server "works", and why a site with no matching binding returns the default site or a 404.',
          e: 'Name-based virtual hosting depends solely on Host (or TLS SNI for 443, which must match the certificate). Server-side, request routing: match binding/server_name, map path to document root, check default document, apply access rules, serve or 404. Access log line per request; error log for 5xx and config problems.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'Set it up', p: [
        { s: 'Do this on the machine whose address is in the A record. Install the web server, make a small page, open the firewall.',
          m: 'On the host at 192.168.110.20: install the role/package, replace the default page with your own index.html, open TCP 80 on the host firewall, and test locally with a browser or curl before testing by name from a client.',
          e: 'Install, confirm the listener with <code>netstat</code>/<code>ss</code>, place content in the document root with permissions the service account can read (IIS_IUSRS on IIS; on RHEL, correct SELinux context httpd_sys_content_t), open TCP 80 (and later 443), test locally by IP, then by name from a client.' }
      ], os: {
        win: {
          intro: { s: 'Windows Server\'s web server is called IIS. Add the role, then put your page in C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot.', m: '<b>IIS</b> (Internet Information Services). Server Manager installs it; the IIS Manager console (inetmgr) manages sites and bindings. The default site serves <code>C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot</code> on port 80 immediately after install, and the firewall rule is added for you.', e: 'IIS 10. Role Web-Server with defaults (static content, default document, logging). Default Web Site bound to *:80. Firewall: "World Wide Web Services (HTTP Traffic-In)" is enabled by the role install. WebAdministration / IISAdministration modules for PowerShell.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Server Manager > Add roles and features > tick <b>Web Server (IIS)</b> > Next through the defaults > Install.', m: 'Server Manager > Add Roles and Features > Server Roles > tick <b>Web Server (IIS)</b> > accept the default role services > Install.', e: 'Install the role with management tools:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Install-WindowsFeature Web-Server -IncludeManagementTools' } },
            { text: { s: 'Open a browser on the server and go to <b>http://localhost/</b>. The blue IIS welcome page means the server is running.', m: 'Test locally: <code>http://localhost/</code> shows the IIS start page (iisstart.htm). That proves the service and the binding; nothing is on the network yet.', e: 'Local test and listener check:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Invoke-WebRequest http://localhost/ -UseBasicParsing | Select StatusCode\nnetstat -ano | findstr :80' }, out: { s: '', m: '', e: 'StatusCode\n----------\n       200\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:80    0.0.0.0:0    LISTENING    4' } },
            { text: { s: 'Open <b>C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot</b>, delete iisstart.htm and iisstart.png, and save your own page as <b>index.html</b>.', m: 'Replace the default content in <code>C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot</code> with your <code>index.html</code>. IIS serves index.html as a default document out of the box (the list is in IIS Manager > Default Document).', e: 'Content:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Remove-Item C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot\\iisstart.*\nSet-Content C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot\\index.html "<h1>www.lab.local</h1><p>Served by IIS on $env:COMPUTERNAME</p>"' } },
            { text: { s: 'Optional: in IIS Manager, right-click <b>Default Web Site</b> > Edit Bindings > Add: type http, host name <b>www.lab.local</b>. Now the site answers to that name.', m: 'Bindings (IIS Manager > Sites > Default Web Site > Bindings): add an http binding with host name <code>www.lab.local</code>. Leave the original *:80 binding too, so tests by IP still work. A second site with a different host name shows name-based hosting.', e: 'Host-name binding:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'New-WebBinding -Name "Default Web Site" -Protocol http -Port 80 -HostHeader www.lab.local' } },
            { text: { s: 'Check the firewall: the IIS install turns on the rule for port 80. In Windows Defender Firewall > Allowed apps, "World Wide Web Services (HTTP)" should be ticked.', m: 'Firewall: the role install enables the inbound rule <b>World Wide Web Services (HTTP Traffic-In)</b>. Verify it, and enable the HTTPS one when you add a certificate later.', e: 'Firewall verification:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup "World Wide Web Services (HTTP)" | Select DisplayName, Enabled' } }
          ],
          after: { s: 'From another machine, browse to the server\'s IP first, then to www.lab.local.', m: 'Test by IP from a client (<code>http://192.168.110.20/</code>) and then by name. Logs: <code>C:\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\W3SVC1\\</code>, one line per request with the status code.', e: 'W3C logs under C:\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\W3SVC1; <code>Get-Website</code>, <code>Get-WebBinding</code>; <code>iisreset</code> restarts. Application pool identity ApplicationPoolIdentity; content ACL must grant IIS_IUSRS read.' }
        },
        ubuntu: {
          intro: { s: 'Ubuntu\'s usual web server is nginx (say "engine-x"). Install it, put your page in /var/www/html, done.', m: '<b>nginx</b>: small, fast, one config file per site in <code>/etc/nginx/sites-available</code>, enabled by symlink into <code>sites-enabled</code>. Document root <code>/var/www/html</code>. Apache (<code>apache2</code>) works the same way with different file names.', e: 'nginx 1.24 on Ubuntu 24.04. Main config /etc/nginx/nginx.conf includes sites-enabled/*. Default server block listens on 80 with root /var/www/html. Runs as www-data. Apache alternative: apache2, a2ensite, /etc/apache2/sites-available.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Install nginx. It starts by itself.', m: 'Install; the service is enabled and started by the package.', e: 'Install and verify the listener:' }, cmd: 'sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y nginx\nsystemctl is-active nginx\nsudo ss -ltnp | grep :80', out: 'active\nLISTEN 0 511 0.0.0.0:80 0.0.0.0:* users:(("nginx",pid=1234,fd=6),...)' },
            { text: { s: 'Test on the server itself.', m: 'Local test with curl: the default "Welcome to nginx!" page.', e: 'Local test:' }, cmd: 'curl -sI http://localhost/ | head -3', out: 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nServer: nginx/1.24.0 (Ubuntu)\nContent-Type: text/html' },
            { text: { s: 'Replace the default page with your own.', m: 'Write your <code>index.html</code> into the document root. Remove the default so it cannot mask yours (the default is index.nginx-debian.html, which nginx prefers).', e: 'Content:' }, cmd: 'sudo rm /var/www/html/index.nginx-debian.html\necho "<h1>www.lab.local</h1><p>Served by nginx on $(hostname)</p>" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html' },
            { text: { s: 'Optional: give the site its name. Create a small config file, enable it, reload.', m: 'Name-based site: a server block whose <code>server_name</code> is www.lab.local. Keep the default site so tests by IP still work.', e: 'Server block for the name:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/www.lab.local', out: 'server {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name www.lab.local intranet.lab.local;\n    root /var/www/html;\n    index index.html;\n    access_log /var/log/nginx/www.lab.local.access.log;\n}' },
            { text: '', cmd: 'sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/www.lab.local /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx', out: 'nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok\nnginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful' },
            { text: { s: 'If the firewall is on, let web traffic through.', m: 'ufw: allow the nginx profile (port 80) if ufw is enabled.', e: 'Firewall:' }, cmd: "sudo ufw allow 'Nginx HTTP'\nsudo ufw status", out: 'Nginx HTTP                 ALLOW       Anywhere' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Watch requests arrive: <code>sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log</code> while a client loads the page.', m: 'Logs: <code>/var/log/nginx/access.log</code> (one line per request: client IP, request, status, bytes) and <code>error.log</code>. <code>nginx -t</code> before every reload.', e: 'access.log combined format; error.log for 403/500 causes (permissions, missing index). Content must be readable by www-data. <code>systemctl reload nginx</code> is zero-downtime.' }
        },
        rhel: {
          intro: { s: 'Alma and Rocky usually run Apache, which the package calls httpd. Install, start, open the firewall, and mind SELinux when you copy files in.', m: '<b>Apache httpd</b>. Config in <code>/etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf</code> and <code>/etc/httpd/conf.d/*.conf</code>; document root <code>/var/www/html</code>. Two extras compared with Ubuntu: firewalld must allow http, and SELinux must see the right label on your files. nginx is available too (<code>dnf install nginx</code>).', e: 'httpd 2.4 on RHEL 9 family. Runs as apache. Default DocumentRoot /var/www/html with no index: the Red Hat test page (welcome.conf) answers 403-with-page until you add content. SELinux: content needs httpd_sys_content_t; files created in /var/www inherit it, files moved from home do not.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Install Apache and start it.', m: 'Install, enable and start the service, confirm the listener.', e: 'Install and verify:' }, cmd: 'sudo dnf install -y httpd\nsudo systemctl enable --now httpd\nsudo ss -ltnp | grep :80', out: 'LISTEN 0 511 *:80 *:* users:(("httpd",pid=1234,fd=4),...)' },
            { text: { s: 'Open the firewall. Until you do, only the server itself can see the page.', m: 'firewalld: allow the http service (and https for later), reload.', e: 'Firewall:' }, cmd: 'sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service={http,https}\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\nsudo firewall-cmd --list-services', out: 'cockpit dhcpv6-client http https ssh' },
            { text: { s: 'Put your page in /var/www/html. Create it there (rather than copying from your home folder) so the security labels come out right.', m: 'Create <code>/var/www/html/index.html</code> in place. If you copy files in from elsewhere, run <code>restorecon</code> afterwards so SELinux lets httpd read them.', e: 'Content with correct SELinux context:' }, cmd: 'echo "<h1>www.lab.local</h1><p>Served by httpd on $(hostname)</p>" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html\nsudo restorecon -Rv /var/www/html\nls -Z /var/www/html/index.html', out: 'unconfined_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0 /var/www/html/index.html' },
            { text: { s: 'Test on the server itself.', m: 'Local test: your page, status 200.', e: 'Local test:' }, cmd: 'curl -sI http://localhost/ | head -3', out: 'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nServer: Apache/2.4.62 (AlmaLinux)\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8' },
            { text: { s: 'Optional: give the site its name with a small config file, check it, and restart.', m: 'Name-based virtual host in <code>/etc/httpd/conf.d/</code>. The default server keeps answering requests by IP.', e: 'VirtualHost:' }, cmd: 'sudo nano /etc/httpd/conf.d/www.lab.local.conf', out: '<VirtualHost *:80>\n    ServerName www.lab.local\n    ServerAlias intranet.lab.local\n    DocumentRoot /var/www/html\n    CustomLog /var/log/httpd/www.lab.local-access.log combined\n</VirtualHost>' },
            { text: '', cmd: 'sudo apachectl configtest && sudo systemctl restart httpd', out: 'Syntax OK' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Watch requests arrive: <code>sudo tail -f /var/log/httpd/access_log</code>.', m: 'Logs in <code>/var/log/httpd/</code>. A 403 with your file present is almost always SELinux: <code>restorecon</code>, or <code>ausearch -m avc -ts recent</code> to see the denial.', e: 'access_log/error_log per vhost as configured. SELinux booleans of interest: httpd_can_network_connect (reverse proxy), httpd_enable_homedirs. Non-standard port needs <code>semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8080</code>.' }
        }
      }}
    ],
    actors: [{ name: 'Browser', addr: '192.168.110.150' }, { name: 'Lab DNS', addr: '192.168.110.10' }, { name: 'Web server', addr: '192.168.110.20' }],
    steps: [
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Address of www.lab.local?"  "192.168.110.20"', m: 'DNS: www.lab.local A?  ->  192.168.110.20', e: 'DNS A/AAAA query, answer 192.168.110.20 (see the DNS row)' } },
      { from: 0, to: 2, label: { s: 'Open a connection (three quick messages)', m: 'TCP three-way handshake to port 80', e: 'SYN / SYN-ACK / ACK  to :80' } },
      { from: 0, to: 2, label: { s: '"GET / for www.lab.local"', m: 'GET / HTTP/1.1  +  Host: www.lab.local', e: 'GET / HTTP/1.1, Host: www.lab.local, Accept, User-Agent' } },
      { from: 2, to: 0, label: { s: '"200 OK" + the page', m: '200 OK  Content-Type: text/html  + index.html', e: '200 OK, Content-Type text/html, Content-Length, body' } },
      { from: 0, to: 2, label: { s: '"GET /logo.png"  (same connection)', m: 'GET /logo.png  (same connection, keep-alive)', e: 'GET /logo.png on the persistent connection' } },
      { from: 2, to: 0, label: { s: '"200 OK" + the picture', m: '200 OK  Content-Type: image/png', e: '200 OK, image/png, Content-Length' } },
      { from: 0, to: 2, dashed: true, label: { s: 'Later: "GET /missing.html"  "404 Not Found"', m: 'GET /missing.html  ->  404 Not Found', e: 'GET /missing.html -> 404, logged in access log' } }
    ],
    checkIntro: [
      { s: 'From a client: first by address, then by name. If the address works and the name does not, the problem is DNS, not the web server.',
        m: 'Test in two steps from a client. By IP proves the server, the firewall and the content. By name proves the A record and the client\'s resolver. Keep them separate and troubleshooting is quick.',
        e: 'Client checks: by IP (server/firewall/content), by name (resolution + Host header routing), headers (server software, content type, length), a deliberate 404, and the server-side access log line for each request.' }
    ],
    check: [
      { os: 'any', cmd: 'http://192.168.110.20/      then      http://www.lab.local/', out: '(your page in the browser)',
        why: { s: 'Open both in a browser. Press F12 > Network to see the request and the 200 code.', m: 'Browser first; then F12 > Network tab, reload, click the first row: request headers (Host), response headers (Server, Content-Type), status 200.', e: 'DevTools Network: check Host in request headers, status, Content-Type, Content-Length, and the Timing tab: DNS lookup, initial connection, TTFB.' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'Invoke-WebRequest http://www.lab.local/ -UseBasicParsing | Select StatusCode, @{n="Server";e={$_.Headers.Server}}, @{n="Length";e={$_.RawContentLength}}', out: 'StatusCode Server                    Length\n---------- ------                    ------\n       200 Microsoft-IIS/10.0            61',
        why: { s: '200 means the page came back. The Server column says which software answered, a quick way to know you hit the right box.', m: 'Status 200 and the Server header identify the responding software (IIS, nginx, Apache). Try a wrong path to see 404.', e: 'Add <code>-Headers @{Host="intranet.lab.local"}</code> to test name-based bindings without touching DNS. <code>Test-NetConnection 192.168.110.20 -Port 80</code> isolates the TCP layer (TcpTestSucceeded).' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'Test-NetConnection www.lab.local -Port 80', out: 'ComputerName     : www.lab.local\nRemoteAddress    : 192.168.110.20\nRemotePort       : 80\nTcpTestSucceeded : True',
        why: { s: 'This only checks that the door (port 80) is open, without asking for a page. If this fails, it is the firewall or the service, not your HTML.', m: 'Separates layers: RemoteAddress shows DNS worked; TcpTestSucceeded shows the port is open (service running, firewall open). False with the right address = firewall or service.', e: 'Pure TCP connect test; no HTTP. Combine with the server\'s <code>netstat -ano | findstr :80</code> / <code>ss -ltnp</code>: listening but unreachable = firewall; not listening = service.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'curl -v http://www.lab.local/ 2>&1 | grep -E "^(\\*  Trying|> |< )"', out: '*  Trying 192.168.110.20:80...\n> GET / HTTP/1.1\n> Host: www.lab.local\n> User-Agent: curl/8.5.0\n> Accept: */*\n< HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n< Server: nginx/1.24.0 (Ubuntu)\n< Content-Type: text/html\n< Content-Length: 61',
        why: { s: 'Lines starting with > are what the browser sends; < are what the server answers. You can read the whole conversation.', m: '<code>-v</code> shows the raw exchange: the resolved address, the request line and Host header, the status line and response headers. This is the same text you would see in a packet capture.', e: '<code>curl -sI</code> for headers only (HEAD); <code>curl -H "Host: intranet.lab.local" http://192.168.110.20/</code> to test a vhost without DNS; <code>curl -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n"</code> for status and timing.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'curl -sI http://www.lab.local/nope.html | head -1\nsudo tail -2 /var/log/nginx/access.log', out: 'HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\n192.168.110.150 - - [14/Sep/2026:09:20:11 -0600] "GET / HTTP/1.1" 200 61 "-" "curl/8.5.0"\n192.168.110.150 - - [14/Sep/2026:09:20:30 -0600] "GET /nope.html HTTP/1.1" 404 162 "-" "curl/8.5.0"',
        why: { s: 'Ask for a page that does not exist to see a 404, then look at the server\'s log: every request is written down with its status.', m: 'A deliberate 404, then the access log on the server (run that part on the server): client IP, request, status, bytes. IIS writes the same to <code>C:\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\W3SVC1</code>; httpd to <code>/var/log/httpd/access_log</code>.', e: 'Combined log format. A request that never appears in the log never reached the server (DNS, routing, firewall); one that appears with 403/404/500 is a server-side config or content problem.' } }
    ],
    protocolRow: 'http', protocolRowTitle: 'HTTP',
    breaks: [
      { s: '<b>Works by IP, not by name.</b> Not a web problem: the A record or the client\'s DNS server is wrong. Go back to the DNS row.', m: '<b>IP works, name fails.</b> The web server is fine. Resolve the name from the client (<code>nslookup www.lab.local</code>): wrong server (DHCP option 6), missing record, or stale cache (<code>ipconfig /flushdns</code>).', e: '<b>Resolution failure:</b> ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED in the browser. Diagnose with nslookup/dig against the intended server; check option 6 and client cache. Not an HTTP issue.' },
      { s: '<b>Page never loads, no error for a long time, then "took too long".</b> The door is closed: the firewall on the server, or the service is not running.', m: '<b>Timeout (ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT).</b> Packets are dropped: host firewall (firewalld on Alma/Rocky is the classic), or wrong address. <code>Test-NetConnection -Port 80</code> fails. Open the port or fix the A record.', e: '<b>SYN dropped:</b> no SYN-ACK. firewalld/ufw/Windows Firewall on the server, or an address that is not the server. <code>ss -ltnp</code> confirms a listener exists; a capture on the server shows whether the SYN arrives.' },
      { s: '<b>Instant "connection refused".</b> The machine is there but nothing is listening on port 80: the web server is stopped.', m: '<b>ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED.</b> The host answered with a TCP reset: reachable, but no service on port 80. Start it (<code>systemctl start nginx</code> / httpd, or IIS Manager > Start) and check it survived a reboot (<code>systemctl enable</code>).', e: '<b>RST on SYN:</b> nothing bound to :80. Service down, crashed on a config error (<code>nginx -t</code>, <code>apachectl configtest</code>, IIS event log), or bound only to 127.0.0.1.' },
      { s: '<b>403 Forbidden.</b> The server found the folder but will not show it: no index.html, or it is not allowed to read your file (on Alma/Rocky, SELinux labels).', m: '<b>403.</b> Directory with no default document (IIS/httpd), or the service account cannot read the file: NTFS permissions for IIS_IUSRS, file mode for www-data/apache, or SELinux context on RHEL (<code>restorecon -Rv /var/www/html</code>).', e: '<b>403:</b> autoindex off + no index; permission denied (check error log); SELinux AVC (<code>ausearch -m avc -ts recent</code>); IIS handler/authorization rules. The error log names the exact cause.' },
      { s: '<b>404 Not Found.</b> Wrong path or file name. Names are case-sensitive on Linux: Index.html is not index.html.', m: '<b>404.</b> The path does not map to a file in the document root. Check spelling and case (Linux is case-sensitive), and that you edited the right site\'s root (the vhost may use a different DocumentRoot).', e: '<b>404:</b> wrong DocumentRoot for the matched vhost/binding, case mismatch, or the request matched the default server rather than the named one. Log shows which vhost logged it.' },
      { s: '<b>Wrong website appears.</b> Several sites on one server and the name you typed did not match any of them, so you got the default one.', m: '<b>Default site served instead of yours.</b> The Host header did not match any binding/server_name/ServerName, so the server used its default. Fix the host name in the site config, or the name you typed.', e: '<b>Vhost fallthrough:</b> nginx picks default_server or the first block; httpd the first VirtualHost; IIS the binding with no host header. Verify with <code>curl -H "Host: ..."</code>.' }
    ]
  },
  /* ===================================================================== */
  {
    id: 'adds',
    group: 'id',
    title: 'AD DS',
    subtitle: 'Active Directory: users, computers and policy',
    chip: 'many ports',
    ports: { s: 'Lots: 53 (DNS), 88 (Kerberos), 389 (LDAP), 445 (file sharing) and more.', m: 'DNS 53, Kerberos 88, LDAP 389 / LDAPS 636, SMB 445, Global Catalog 3268, RPC 135 + dynamic, NTP 123.', e: 'TCP/UDP 53 DNS; 88 Kerberos; 389 LDAP, 636 LDAPS, 3268/3269 GC; 445 SMB (SYSVOL, NETLOGON); 135 RPC EPM + 49152-65535 dynamic; UDP 123 NTP; 464 kpasswd; UDP 389 CLDAP (DC locator ping).' },
    winRole: 'Active Directory Domain Services',
    linuxPkgs: 'Ubuntu: samba (AD DC) + krb5-user. Alma/Rocky: realmd, sssd, adcli (join a domain; no DC role in the repos)',
    answers: { s: '"Who is this user, and what may they do?"', m: 'Authentication (Kerberos), directory lookups (LDAP) and policy (Group Policy) for every user and computer in the domain', e: 'Kerberos tickets, LDAP directory objects (users, computers, groups, OUs), DC location via DNS SRV, Group Policy via SMB' },
    oneLiner: {
      s: 'Active Directory is the one place a company keeps its list of people and computers, so you can log in to any machine with the same name and password.',
      m: 'AD DS is a directory of users, computers, groups and policies, held on domain controllers, that lets any domain-joined machine authenticate any domain user with one account and apply settings centrally.',
      e: 'AD DS is a multi-master LDAP directory with a Kerberos KDC on each domain controller, located by clients through DNS SRV records, replicating a partitioned database and delivering Group Policy through SYSVOL over SMB.'
    },
    project: {
      s: 'The domain is the frame around everything else: the DNS server with your A record normally lives on the domain controller, and the web server and your PC join the domain so one account logs in everywhere.',
      m: 'In the project the domain controller is also the DNS server, so the <code>lab.local</code> zone with your A record becomes an <b>AD-integrated zone</b>. The DHCP server is authorized in the domain. The web server and the client are joined, so a domain user can log on to either and IIS can use Windows authentication.',
      e: 'DC = DNS (AD-integrated zone lab.local, secure dynamic updates) = DHCP (authorized). Members: web server (computer object, optional Kerberos-authenticated IIS) and client. Verify DC location via SRV lookups, Kerberos ticket acquisition, LDAP search, and SYSVOL access. Time sync is a hard dependency (5-minute Kerberos skew).'
    },
    sections: [
      { h: 'What it does', p: [
        { s: 'Without a directory, every computer keeps its own list of users, so you would need a separate account on each one. Active Directory keeps <b>one</b> list, on special servers called <b>domain controllers</b>. Any computer that has <b>joined the domain</b> asks a domain controller "is this person who they say they are?" and follows the answer.',
          m: 'Every Windows machine has local accounts. A <b>domain</b> replaces those with central accounts stored on <b>domain controllers</b> (DCs). Machines <b>join</b> the domain (getting their own computer account), users log on with <code>LAB\\alice</code> or <code>alice@lab.local</code>, and the DC does the authentication. Admins organise objects in <b>organisational units</b> (OUs) and attach <b>Group Policy</b> to them to configure hundreds of machines at once.',
          e: 'A domain is a security boundary and replication partition; a forest is one or more domains sharing a schema and a Global Catalog. Every DC holds a writable copy (multi-master) except for FSMO roles. Objects: users, computers, groups (security/distribution; domain local/global/universal), OUs, GPOs. Authentication is Kerberos (NTLM as fallback); authorization is via group SIDs in the ticket\'s PAC.' },
        { s: 'Active Directory cannot work without DNS. A computer finds its domain controller by asking DNS "who provides logon for lab.local?" That is why the DNS server almost always runs on the domain controller.',
          m: '<b>DNS is how clients find DCs.</b> When a DC is promoted it registers <b>SRV records</b> such as <code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local</code> in the zone. A client resolves those, then talks Kerberos and LDAP to the DC it picked. Break DNS and nobody can log on, which is why the DC is normally the DNS server and every domain member must use it.',
          e: 'DC locator: client queries <code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.&lt;domain&gt;</code> (and site-specific variants under <code>_sites</code>), pings candidates with CLDAP (UDP 389), picks a DC in its site. Netlogon registers/refreshes these SRV records via dynamic update, so the zone must allow secure dynamic updates. The <code>_msdcs</code> subzone is forest-wide.' },
        { s: 'Logging on uses <b>Kerberos</b>: you prove who you are once to the domain controller and get a "ticket", then show tickets to other servers instead of typing your password again. That is what "single sign-on" means.',
          m: '<b>Kerberos</b> gives single sign-on. At logon the client proves it knows the password to the DC (the <b>KDC</b>) and gets a <b>ticket-granting ticket</b> (TGT). For each server it wants (a file share, IIS with Windows auth) it exchanges the TGT for a <b>service ticket</b> and presents that. Passwords never cross the network; tickets expire (10 h by default). Clocks must agree within 5 minutes or Kerberos refuses.',
          e: 'AS-REQ/AS-REP (pre-auth timestamp encrypted with the user\'s key, returns TGT + session key), TGS-REQ/TGS-REP (service ticket for an SPN, e.g. <code>HTTP/www.lab.local</code>), AP-REQ to the service. PAC carries group SIDs. Encryption AES256 by default; RC4 deprecated. Skew tolerance 5 min; ticket lifetime 10 h, renewable 7 d. NTLM remains for IP-address access and non-domain hosts.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'How it works: joining and logging on', diagram: true, p: [
        { s: 'A domain PC boots, finds a domain controller through DNS, then the user logs on: prove who you are, get a ticket, use the ticket for everything else. Finally the PC fetches its settings (Group Policy) from the domain controller\'s shared folder.',
          m: 'Sequence at logon: DNS SRV lookup to find a DC (1, 2), Kerberos AS exchange for the TGT (3, 4), TGS exchange for a service ticket, here for the DC\'s own LDAP service (5, 6), LDAP queries for the user\'s and computer\'s group memberships and GPO links (7), then SMB to <code>\\\\lab.local\\SYSVOL</code> to read the policies (8). Every step uses a different port, which is why the DC needs so many open.',
          e: 'DC locator via SRV + CLDAP; AS-REQ with PA-ENC-TIMESTAMP, AS-REP with TGT; TGS-REQ for <code>ldap/dc1.lab.local</code>, TGS-REP; LDAP bind (SASL/GSSAPI) and searches under the domain NC for tokenGroups and gPLink; SMB2 to SYSVOL for GPT.INI and policy files, NETLOGON for scripts. Machine account performs its own equivalent at boot before the user logs on.' }
      ], after: [
        { s: 'Joining the domain happens once, before any of that: an admin types their domain password on the PC, the PC creates its own account on the domain controller, and reboots.',
          m: 'The <b>join</b> is a one-time setup: with domain admin credentials the PC creates its computer account (<code>PC1$</code>) in the Computers container, sets a machine password, and adds itself to the domain. From then on the machine itself authenticates with Kerberos before any user does.',
          e: 'Join: LDAP add of the computer object (or pre-staged), sets sAMAccountName PC1$, servicePrincipalNames, dNSHostName; machine password rotates every 30 days by default. Netlogon secure channel is established with the machine account; <code>Test-ComputerSecureChannel</code> checks it. Offline domain join (djoin) exists for imaging.' }
      ]},
      { h: 'Set it up', p: [
        { s: 'Windows Server is the real thing here; Ubuntu can play the same part with Samba; Alma and Rocky do not ship the domain-controller software, so on those you join a computer to the domain instead.',
          m: 'Windows Server: promote a server to the first DC of a new forest (it installs DNS as part of it). Ubuntu: <b>Samba</b> can be a fully functional AD DC that Windows clients join. Alma/Rocky: the repos have no Samba DC (RHEL builds Samba against MIT Kerberos, which does not support the DC role), so the natural exercise is to <b>join</b> the Linux box to the domain with realmd and sssd.', 
          e: 'Windows: Install-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services, Install-ADDSForest (DNS role installed and zone created AD-integrated). Samba: <code>samba-tool domain provision</code> with the internal DNS or BIND9_DLZ back end, Heimdal Kerberos. RHEL family: samba-dc packages are absent (MIT KDC limitation); use realmd + sssd + adcli for membership, or build Samba from source / use a third-party repo if a DC is required.' }
      ], os: {
        win: {
          intro: { s: 'Give the server a fixed address and a good name, add the role, promote it to a domain controller, reboot, then create a user.', m: 'Prerequisites: static IP, sensible hostname (DC1), the server\'s DNS pointing at itself after promotion. The wizard installs DNS and creates the AD-integrated zone. Expect one reboot.', e: 'Rename, static addressing, role install, forest promotion with DSRM password, reboot; then verify SRV registration, create OU/user, authorize DHCP, and confirm the DNS zone is AD-integrated (the earlier standalone zone, if any, can be converted: zone Properties > Type > Store in AD).' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Rename the computer to <b>DC1</b> and give it a fixed address (its own address as DNS server). Restart.', m: 'Hostname DC1, static 192.168.110.10/24, gateway .1, DNS pointing at itself. Rename requires a restart.', e: 'Name and addressing:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Rename-Computer -NewName DC1 -Restart\n# after restart:\nNew-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -IPAddress 192.168.110.10 -PrefixLength 24 -DefaultGateway 192.168.110.1\nSet-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -ServerAddresses 127.0.0.1' } },
            { text: { s: 'Server Manager > Add roles and features > tick <b>Active Directory Domain Services</b> (say yes to the extra features it asks for) > Install.', m: 'Server Manager > Add Roles and Features > tick <b>Active Directory Domain Services</b>, accept the required features (includes the AD PowerShell module and GPMC) > Install. Do not reboot yet.', e: 'Role install:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Install-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services -IncludeManagementTools' } },
            { text: { s: 'Click the yellow flag > <b>Promote this server to a domain controller</b>. Choose <b>Add a new forest</b>, root domain name <b>lab.local</b>. Keep DNS server ticked, type a recovery (DSRM) password, accept the rest, Install. It reboots by itself.', m: 'Notification flag > <b>Promote this server to a domain controller</b> > Add a new forest > root domain <code>lab.local</code> > functional levels default > tick DNS server and Global Catalog > DSRM password > ignore the delegation warning (there is no parent zone) > NetBIOS name LAB > default paths > Install. Automatic reboot.', e: 'Promotion:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Install-ADDSForest -DomainName lab.local -DomainNetbiosName LAB -InstallDns -SafeModeAdministratorPassword (Read-Host -AsSecureString "DSRM password") -Force' } },
            { text: { s: 'After the reboot, log on as <b>LAB\\Administrator</b>. Open Tools > <b>DNS</b>: the lab.local zone now has a folder full of underscore names. Those are the records computers use to find this domain controller.', m: 'Log on as LAB\\Administrator. DNS console: <code>lab.local</code> is now AD-integrated and contains <code>_msdcs</code>, <code>_sites</code>, <code>_tcp</code>, <code>_udp</code> with SRV records. Add the A record for www here if you did not already (DNS row). Set forwarders on this server.', e: 'Verify SRV registration and health:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Resolve-DnsName _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local -Type SRV\ndcdiag /test:dns /test:advertising\nGet-ADDomainController | Select HostName, IsGlobalCatalog, OperationMasterRoles' } },
            { text: { s: 'Tools > <b>Active Directory Users and Computers</b>. Right-click lab.local > New > <b>Organizational Unit</b> called <b>Students</b>. Right-click Students > New > <b>User</b>: alice, logon name alice, a password, untick "must change password" for the lab.', m: 'ADUC (dsa.msc): create OU <b>Students</b>, then a user <b>alice</b> in it (UPN alice@lab.local). Optionally a group <b>WebAdmins</b> and add alice. Never create users in the default Users container for real deployments; OUs are what GPOs link to.', e: 'OU, user, group:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'New-ADOrganizationalUnit -Name Students -Path "DC=lab,DC=local"\nNew-ADUser -Name "Alice Lee" -SamAccountName alice -UserPrincipalName alice@lab.local -Path "OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local" -AccountPassword (Read-Host -AsSecureString "Password") -Enabled $true\nNew-ADGroup -Name WebAdmins -GroupScope Global -Path "OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local"\nAdd-ADGroupMember -Identity WebAdmins -Members alice' } },
            { text: { s: 'Join the web server and a client PC: Settings > System > About > <b>Domain or workgroup</b> > Change > Domain: <b>lab.local</b> > type LAB\\Administrator and the password > restart. Both machines must use the DC as their DNS server, which DHCP option 6 already does for the client.', m: 'Join members: they must resolve <code>lab.local</code> through the DC (DHCP option 6 = .10, or set it statically on the web server). System Properties > Change > Domain lab.local > credentials > restart. Then log on as <code>LAB\\alice</code> on the client.', e: 'Join from the member (PowerShell):' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-Computer -DomainName lab.local -Credential LAB\\Administrator -Restart' } },
            { text: { s: 'If your DHCP server is this same machine or another Windows box, make sure it is authorized now that a domain exists (DHCP console: green tick on IPv4).', m: 'DHCP authorization: in a domain, a Windows DHCP server must be authorized (DHCP console > right-click server > Authorize) or it stops answering.', e: 'Authorize DHCP in AD:' }, cmd: { s: '', m: '', e: 'Add-DhcpServerInDC -DnsName dc1.lab.local -IPAddress 192.168.110.10\nGet-DhcpServerInDC' } }
          ],
          after: { s: 'A first Group Policy to try: Tools > Group Policy Management > right-click Students > Create a GPO, edit it, and set a desktop wallpaper or a logon message. Log off and on with alice to see it.', m: 'Group Policy: GPMC (gpmc.msc) > link a new GPO to the Students OU, e.g. User Configuration > Policies > Administrative Templates > Desktop > Desktop > Desktop Wallpaper. <code>gpupdate /force</code> then log on as alice. <code>gpresult /r</code> shows what applied.', e: 'GPO applied at next refresh (90 min +/- 30) or <code>gpupdate /force</code>; <code>gpresult /h report.html</code> for RSoP. Time: the PDC emulator should sync to an external NTP source (<code>w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:"pool.ntp.org" /syncfromflags:manual /reliable:yes /update</code>); members sync from the domain hierarchy automatically.' }
        },
        ubuntu: {
          intro: { s: 'Samba turns an Ubuntu server into a domain controller that Windows PCs can join, with the same domain name, users and DNS records.', m: '<b>Samba AD DC</b>: provision a new domain with <code>samba-tool</code>. Samba runs its own DNS server for the zone (internal back end) and its own Kerberos KDC. Manage users with samba-tool, or from a Windows machine with RSAT (ADUC works against Samba).', e: 'Samba 4.19 on Ubuntu 24.04. Provision with SAMBA_INTERNAL DNS (simplest) or BIND9_DLZ (if BIND already runs here). Heimdal KDC. Disable systemd-resolved\'s stub or point it at Samba. Functional level 2008R2 by default (2016 in newer versions). SYSVOL replication between multiple Samba DCs is not automatic (rsync/osync needed).' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Set the hostname to dc1 with the full name dc1.lab.local, a fixed address, and install Samba and the Kerberos tools.', m: 'Hostname <code>dc1</code>, FQDN <code>dc1.lab.local</code> in /etc/hosts, static IP. Install Samba, winbind, and krb5-user (when asked for a default realm, type <code>LAB.LOCAL</code> in capitals).', e: 'Prerequisites and packages:' }, cmd: 'sudo hostnamectl set-hostname dc1\necho "192.168.110.10 dc1.lab.local dc1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install -y samba krb5-user winbind libpam-winbind libnss-winbind smbclient' },
            { text: { s: 'Stop the ordinary file-sharing services and remove the default settings file: the domain controller mode replaces them.', m: 'Disable the stand-alone smbd/nmbd/winbind units (the AD DC uses a single <code>samba-ad-dc</code> service) and move the default smb.conf out of the way, since provisioning writes a new one.', e: 'Disable member-mode units, clear config:' }, cmd: 'sudo systemctl disable --now smbd nmbd winbind\nsudo systemctl unmask samba-ad-dc\nsudo mv /etc/samba/smb.conf /etc/samba/smb.conf.orig' },
            { text: { s: 'Create the domain. The tool asks a few questions; the defaults are right except the Administrator password.', m: 'Provision interactively: realm <code>LAB.LOCAL</code>, domain <code>LAB</code>, role dc, DNS backend SAMBA_INTERNAL, DNS forwarder your upstream resolver, Administrator password (complex: upper, lower, digit, 8+).', e: 'Provision (non-interactive form shown):' }, cmd: "sudo samba-tool domain provision --use-rfc2307 --realm=LAB.LOCAL --domain=LAB --server-role=dc --dns-backend=SAMBA_INTERNAL --option='dns forwarder = 1.1.1.1' --adminpass='Str0ngPassw0rd!'", out: 'Setting up self join\nAdding DNS accounts\n...\nOnce the above files are installed, your Samba AD server will be ready to use\nServer Role:           active directory domain controller\nHostname:              dc1\nNetBIOS Domain:        LAB\nDNS Domain:            lab.local\nDOMAIN SID:            S-1-5-21-...' },
            { text: { s: 'Make the server use its own DNS, use the Kerberos settings Samba wrote, and start the domain controller service.', m: 'Point the resolver at Samba (disable systemd-resolved\'s stub listener so port 53 is free), copy Samba\'s krb5.conf into place, then enable and start <code>samba-ad-dc</code>.', e: 'Resolver, Kerberos config, start:' }, cmd: 'sudo sed -i "s/^#\\?DNSStubListener=.*/DNSStubListener=no/" /etc/systemd/resolved.conf\nsudo systemctl restart systemd-resolved\nsudo resolvectl dns ens18 127.0.0.1 && sudo resolvectl domain ens18 lab.local\nsudo cp /var/lib/samba/private/krb5.conf /etc/krb5.conf\nsudo systemctl enable --now samba-ad-dc\nsystemctl is-active samba-ad-dc', out: 'active' },
            { text: { s: 'Check that the domain answers: look up the record that tells computers where the domain controller is, and get a Kerberos ticket as Administrator.', m: 'Verify the SRV record and Kerberos. <code>klist</code> should show a ticket for <code>krbtgt/LAB.LOCAL</code>.', e: 'Verification:' }, cmd: "host -t SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local\nkinit administrator@LAB.LOCAL && klist\nsamba-tool domain level show", out: '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local has SRV record 0 100 389 dc1.lab.local.\nPassword for administrator@LAB.LOCAL:\nTicket cache: FILE:/tmp/krb5cc_1000\nDefault principal: administrator@LAB.LOCAL\nValid starting     Expires            Service principal\n14/09/26 09:30:01  14/09/26 19:30:01  krbtgt/LAB.LOCAL@LAB.LOCAL\nDomain and forest function level for domain \'DC=lab,DC=local\'\nForest function level: (Windows) 2008 R2\nDomain function level: (Windows) 2008 R2' },
            { text: { s: 'Create an organisational unit and a user, and add the A record for the web server into the domain\'s DNS.', m: 'Users and OUs with samba-tool; DNS records with <code>samba-tool dns</code> (the zone lab.local already exists, created by provisioning).', e: 'Objects and DNS records:' }, cmd: "sudo samba-tool ou create 'OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local'\nsudo samba-tool user create alice 'Passw0rd!' --given-name=Alice --surname=Lee --userou='OU=Students'\nsudo samba-tool group add WebAdmins && sudo samba-tool group addmembers WebAdmins alice\nsudo samba-tool dns add dc1.lab.local lab.local www A 192.168.110.20 -U administrator\nsudo samba-tool dns add dc1.lab.local lab.local @ MX 'mail.lab.local 10' -U administrator", out: 'User \'alice\' added successfully\nAdded group WebAdmins\nAdded members to group WebAdmins\nRecord added successfully\nRecord added successfully' }
          ],
          after: { s: 'Windows PCs join this domain exactly as they would a Windows domain controller: System > Change > Domain lab.local.', m: 'Windows clients join normally (they must use 192.168.110.10 as DNS, which DHCP option 6 provides). From a joined Windows machine, RSAT\'s ADUC and Group Policy Management work against the Samba DC. <code>samba-tool dns query dc1.lab.local lab.local @ ALL -U administrator</code> lists the zone.', e: 'Time: Samba DC should serve NTP (chrony with <code>ntpsigndsocket</code>) for members. Reverse zone: <code>samba-tool dns zonecreate dc1.lab.local 110.168.192.in-addr.arpa -U administrator</code>. Logs in /var/log/samba; <code>samba-tool dbcheck</code>; for Linux clients use sssd or winbind membership.' }
        },
        rhel: {
          intro: { s: 'Alma and Rocky cannot be the domain controller out of the box, so here the exercise is the other side: joining a Linux server (your web server, say) to the domain so domain users can log on to it.', m: 'RHEL-family repos have no Samba DC role. Instead, <b>join</b> the Alma/Rocky box (for example the web server) to the domain with <b>realmd</b> and <b>sssd</b>: domain users can then log in over SSH, and <code>id alice</code> resolves through the DC.', e: 'realmd discovers the domain via SRV, adcli creates the computer object and keytab, sssd provides NSS/PAM for domain identities using Kerberos/LDAP against the DC. Requires the host to use the DC for DNS and to have time in sync. Result: a member server, not a DC.' },
          steps: [
            { text: { s: 'Point the machine\'s DNS at the domain controller and check the clock is right. Kerberos refuses to work if the time is off by more than five minutes.', m: 'Use the DC as DNS (nmcli or DHCP option 6) and make sure chrony is running and synced. Kerberos skew limit is 5 minutes.', e: 'DNS and time prerequisites:' }, cmd: 'sudo nmcli con mod "ens18" ipv4.dns 192.168.110.10 && sudo nmcli con up "ens18"\nsudo systemctl enable --now chronyd && chronyc tracking | head -3\nhost -t SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local', out: 'Reference ID    : C0A86E0A (dc1.lab.local)\nStratum         : 4\nRef time (UTC)  : Mon Sep 14 15:31:02 2026\n_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local has SRV record 0 100 389 dc1.lab.local.' },
            { text: { s: 'Install the joining tools.', m: 'Install realmd, sssd and helpers.', e: 'Packages:' }, cmd: 'sudo dnf install -y realmd sssd sssd-tools adcli oddjob oddjob-mkhomedir samba-common-tools krb5-workstation' },
            { text: { s: 'Ask realmd what it can see. It should find lab.local and say what is needed to join.', m: '<code>realm discover</code> reads the SRV records and reports the domain type and required packages.', e: 'Discovery:' }, cmd: 'realm discover lab.local', out: 'lab.local\n  type: kerberos\n  realm-name: LAB.LOCAL\n  domain-name: lab.local\n  configured: no\n  server-software: active-directory\n  client-software: sssd\n  required-package: oddjob\n  required-package: oddjob-mkhomedir\n  required-package: sssd\n  required-package: adcli\n  required-package: samba-common-tools' },
            { text: { s: 'Join, using the domain Administrator password. This creates the computer\'s account on the domain controller.', m: 'Join with a domain admin account; realmd writes sssd.conf and krb5.conf and creates the computer object (in the Computers container unless you pass <code>--computer-ou</code>).', e: 'Join:' }, cmd: 'sudo realm join -U Administrator lab.local --computer-ou="OU=Servers,DC=lab,DC=local"\nrealm list', out: 'Password for Administrator:\nlab.local\n  type: kerberos\n  realm-name: LAB.LOCAL\n  domain-name: lab.local\n  configured: kerberos-member\n  server-software: active-directory\n  client-software: sssd\n  login-formats: %U@lab.local\n  login-policy: allow-realm-logins' },
            { text: { s: 'Check that a domain user is now known to this machine, and let users have home folders made on first login.', m: '<code>id</code> resolves the domain user through sssd; enable mkhomedir so the first SSH login gets a home directory. Optionally restrict who may log in.', e: 'Identity check and PAM home directories:' }, cmd: 'id alice@lab.local\nsudo authselect select sssd with-mkhomedir --force\nsudo systemctl enable --now oddjobd\nsudo realm permit -g WebAdmins@lab.local', out: 'uid=1234801104(alice@lab.local) gid=1234800513(domain users@lab.local) groups=1234800513(domain users@lab.local),1234801105(webadmins@lab.local)' },
            { text: { s: 'Log in as the domain user over SSH from another machine.', m: 'SSH as <code>alice@lab.local</code>; sssd authenticates against the DC with Kerberos and the home directory is created.', e: 'Test:' }, cmd: 'ssh alice@lab.local@192.168.110.20\nklist', out: "alice@lab.local@192.168.110.20's password:\nCreating home directory for alice@lab.local.\nTicket cache: KCM:1234801104\nDefault principal: alice@LAB.LOCAL" }
          ],
          after: { s: 'On the domain controller, the new computer appears in Active Directory Users and Computers. Leaving the domain is one command: <code>realm leave</code>.', m: 'The computer object appears in ADUC. To drop the domain-suffix from usernames set <code>use_fully_qualified_names = False</code> in /etc/sssd/sssd.conf and restart sssd. <code>realm leave</code> removes the membership.', e: 'sssd.conf: use_fully_qualified_names, fallback_homedir, access_provider = ad (GPO-based logon rights via ad_gpo_access_control). Keytab at /etc/krb5.keytab (<code>klist -k</code>). Logs: /var/log/sssd/, <code>sssctl domain-status lab.local</code>.' }
        }
      }}
    ],
    actors: [{ name: 'Client PC', addr: '192.168.110.150' }, { name: 'DC1 (DNS, KDC, LDAP)', addr: '192.168.110.10' }],
    steps: [
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Who handles logons for lab.local?"', m: 'DNS SRV  _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local', e: 'DNS query SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: '"dc1.lab.local does"', m: 'SRV answer: dc1.lab.local 389  (+ A record)', e: 'SRV 0 100 389 dc1.lab.local + A 192.168.110.10; CLDAP ping follows' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"I am alice, here is proof"  (Kerberos)', m: 'Kerberos AS-REQ  (alice, encrypted timestamp)', e: 'AS-REQ  cname alice, PA-ENC-TIMESTAMP, etype AES256  :88' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: '"Here is your ticket for the day" (TGT)', m: 'AS-REP  ticket-granting ticket (TGT)', e: 'AS-REP  TGT (krbtgt/LAB.LOCAL) + session key, 10 h' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Ticket for the directory service, please"', m: 'TGS-REQ  for ldap/dc1.lab.local', e: 'TGS-REQ  sname ldap/dc1.lab.local, presenting TGT' } },
      { from: 1, to: 0, label: { s: '"Here is a ticket for that"', m: 'TGS-REP  service ticket', e: 'TGS-REP  service ticket with PAC (group SIDs)' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"What groups is alice in? What settings apply?"  (LDAP)', m: 'LDAP bind + search  (groups, GPO links)  :389', e: 'LDAP SASL/GSSAPI bind, search tokenGroups, gPLink on OU chain' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, label: { s: '"Give me the policy files"  (file sharing)', m: 'SMB  \\\\lab.local\\SYSVOL  (Group Policy)  :445', e: 'SMB2 tree connect SYSVOL, read Policies\\{GUID}\\GPT.INI, apply' } },
      { from: 0, to: 1, dashed: true, label: { s: 'Later, to any other server: show the ticket, no password', m: 'Later: TGS-REQ for HTTP/www.lab.local, present ticket to IIS', e: 'TGS-REQ HTTP/www.lab.local; AP-REQ in Authorization: Negotiate to IIS' } }
    ],
    checkIntro: [
      { s: 'On a PC that has joined the domain, log on as the domain user and check: which domain controller did it find, and did it get a ticket?',
        m: 'From a joined client logged on as LAB\\alice: confirm the domain and DC found, list Kerberos tickets, query the directory, and check time sync. Each command tests one piece of the diagram.',
        e: 'Verify DC location (SRV + locator), Kerberos ticket acquisition (TGT and service tickets), LDAP access, secure channel, time skew and Group Policy application.' }
    ],
    check: [
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'whoami /fqdn\nnltest /dsgetdc:lab.local', out: 'CN=Alice Lee,OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local\n\n           DC: \\\\DC1.lab.local\n      Address: \\\\192.168.110.10\n     Dom Guid: 3a1f...\n     Dom Name: lab.local\n  Forest Name: lab.local\n Dc Site Name: Default-First-Site-Name\n        Flags: PDC GC DS LDAP KDC TIMESERV WRITABLE DNS_DC DNS_DOMAIN DNS_FOREST CLOSE_SITE FULL_SECRET WS DS_8 DS_9 DS_10\nThe command completed successfully',
        why: { s: 'The first line shows where alice lives in the directory (her OU). The second shows which domain controller the PC found through DNS.', m: '<code>whoami /fqdn</code> proves you are logged on with a domain account and shows its OU path. <code>nltest /dsgetdc</code> runs the DC locator: the DC it found, its address, and its roles (KDC, GC, TIMESERV).', e: 'Distinguished name confirms the object location for GPO scoping. dsgetdc exercises SRV + CLDAP; flags show KDC/GC/PDC. Failure here = DNS (option 6, SRV records) before anything else.' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'klist', out: 'Current LogonId is 0:0x3e7a1\n\nCached Tickets: (2)\n\n#0>  Client: alice @ LAB.LOCAL\n     Server: krbtgt/LAB.LOCAL @ LAB.LOCAL\n     KerbTicket Encryption Type: AES-256-CTS-HMAC-SHA1-96\n     Start Time: 9/14/2026 9:40:12 (local)\n     End Time:   9/14/2026 19:40:12 (local)\n\n#1>  Client: alice @ LAB.LOCAL\n     Server: ldap/DC1.lab.local/lab.local @ LAB.LOCAL',
        why: { s: 'The tickets. The first is the day pass (krbtgt); the others are for individual services. If this list is empty, Kerberos failed and Windows fell back to the old method.', m: 'Ticket #0 is the TGT (krbtgt), valid 10 hours. Others are service tickets, here for the DC\'s LDAP. An empty list after a domain logon means Kerberos failed (time skew, DNS) and NTLM was used instead. <code>klist purge</code> clears them; they come back on next use.', e: 'Verify etype AES-256 (RC4 indicates legacy config). Service tickets appear per SPN as they are used; <code>klist tgt</code> shows TGT details; <code>klist purge</code> forces re-acquisition after group changes (PAC refresh).' } },
      { os: 'win', cmd: 'w32tm /stripchart /computer:dc1.lab.local /samples:3 /dataonly\nTest-ComputerSecureChannel\ngpresult /r | findstr /i "Applied Group"', out: 'Tracking dc1.lab.local [192.168.110.10:123].\n09:41:03, +00.0012340s\n09:41:05, +00.0010871s\n09:41:07, +00.0011502s\nTrue\n    Applied Group Policy Objects\n        Students Wallpaper\n        Default Domain Policy',
        why: { s: 'Three checks: the clock agrees with the domain controller, the PC\'s own account is healthy, and the policies you made actually arrived.', m: 'Time offset must be well under 5 minutes (here about a millisecond). Secure channel True means the computer account works. gpresult lists the GPOs applied, which proves SYSVOL access and OU linking.', e: 'Skew beyond 300 s breaks Kerberos (KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW). <code>Test-ComputerSecureChannel -Repair -Credential LAB\\Administrator</code> fixes a broken machine password. <code>gpresult /h</code> gives RSoP with denied GPOs and reasons.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'host -t SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local\nkinit alice@LAB.LOCAL && klist', out: '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local has SRV record 0 100 389 dc1.lab.local.\nPassword for alice@LAB.LOCAL:\nTicket cache: KCM:1000\nDefault principal: alice@LAB.LOCAL\n\nValid starting       Expires              Service principal\n14/09/2026 09:42:10  14/09/2026 19:42:10  krbtgt/LAB.LOCAL@LAB.LOCAL',
        why: { s: 'The same two steps as the Windows PC: find the domain controller, get a ticket.', m: 'SRV proves DC location; <code>kinit</code> performs the AS exchange and <code>klist</code> shows the TGT. Works from any Linux box with krb5 configured for the realm, joined or not.', e: 'kinit failure codes: "Cannot find KDC" = DNS/krb5.conf realm; "Preauthentication failed" = wrong password; "Clock skew too great" = time. Realm names are upper-case by convention and case-sensitive.' } },
      { os: 'linux', cmd: 'ldapsearch -Y GSSAPI -H ldap://dc1.lab.local -b "DC=lab,DC=local" "(sAMAccountName=alice)" dn memberOf 2>/dev/null\nid alice@lab.local', out: 'dn: CN=Alice Lee,OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local\nmemberOf: CN=WebAdmins,OU=Students,DC=lab,DC=local\n\nuid=1234801104(alice@lab.local) gid=1234800513(domain users@lab.local) groups=1234800513(domain users@lab.local),1234801105(webadmins@lab.local)',
        why: { s: 'Ask the directory directly about alice, using the ticket from the previous step instead of a password. Then see that the Linux machine itself knows who she is.', m: '<code>ldapsearch -Y GSSAPI</code> binds with the Kerberos ticket (no password typed) and reads alice\'s object and groups. <code>id</code> only works on a joined machine: sssd maps the AD user and groups to Unix ids.', e: 'GSSAPI bind proves the ldap/dc1 service ticket path. sssd ID mapping derives uid/gid from SIDs (ldap_id_mapping = True) unless rfc2307 attributes are used. <code>sssctl user-checks alice@lab.local</code> for PAM/NSS diagnostics.' } }
    ],
    breaks: [
      { s: '<b>"An Active Directory Domain Controller could not be contacted" when joining.</b> The PC is not using the domain controller as its DNS server. Fix DNS first; nothing in AD works without it.', m: '<b>Join fails / cannot find DC.</b> Always DNS: the client\'s DNS server is not the DC (DHCP option 6 or a static setting), or the SRV records are missing (<code>nslookup -type=SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.lab.local</code>). Fix DNS, <code>ipconfig /flushdns</code>, retry.', e: '<b>DC locator failure:</b> verify SRV records exist (Netlogon registers them; <code>nltest /dsregdns</code> re-registers), the client resolves through the DC, and UDP 389 CLDAP is not blocked. <code>dcdiag /test:dns</code> on the DC.' },
      { s: '<b>Logon works one day, fails the next with a time error.</b> The PC\'s clock drifted more than five minutes from the domain controller. Fix the time.', m: '<b>Kerberos clock skew.</b> Error mentions time or "the time difference"; the VM snapshot or a dead CMOS battery moved the clock. Domain members sync from the DC automatically (<code>w32tm /resync</code>); the DC itself needs an external NTP source.', e: '<b>KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW:</b> >300 s offset. Members use the domain hierarchy (NT5DS); the PDC emulator should be configured for external NTP. On Linux members, chrony must be running. Reverting VM snapshots is the classic lab cause.' },
      { s: '<b>"The trust relationship between this workstation and the primary domain failed."</b> The PC\'s own account password no longer matches the domain controller\'s copy, usually after restoring an old snapshot. Repair it or rejoin.', m: '<b>Broken secure channel.</b> The machine account password on the PC and in AD differ (snapshot rollback, duplicate names, reimaging). Fix without a rejoin: <code>Test-ComputerSecureChannel -Repair -Credential LAB\\Administrator</code>, or reset the computer account in ADUC and rejoin.', e: '<b>Machine password mismatch:</b> NETLOGON event 3210/5722. <code>Reset-ComputerMachinePassword -Server dc1 -Credential LAB\\Administrator</code> or <code>netdom resetpwd</code>. Disable the 30-day rotation only for snapshot-heavy labs (not production).' },
      { s: '<b>Users can log on but the web server or file share asks for a password again.</b> The service does not have a Kerberos name (SPN) registered, so single sign-on falls back.', m: '<b>No single sign-on to a service.</b> Missing or duplicate SPN (<code>setspn -L</code>), or the site is accessed by IP instead of name (Kerberos needs a name). IIS with Windows auth: SPN <code>HTTP/www.lab.local</code> on the server\'s computer account, and the site in the browser\'s intranet zone.', e: '<b>SPN issues:</b> <code>setspn -Q HTTP/www.lab.local</code>; duplicates break Kerberos silently (event 4769 failure, NTLM fallback). Access by IP always uses NTLM. For a kernel-mode IIS auth the SPN belongs on the machine account; for a custom app-pool identity, on that account.' },
      { s: '<b>Group Policy did not apply.</b> The PC or user is in the wrong OU, or the policy file store on the domain controller cannot be reached.', m: '<b>GPO not applying.</b> Object is not under the OU the GPO is linked to (check with <code>whoami /fqdn</code>), security filtering excludes it, or SYSVOL is unreachable (port 445). <code>gpresult /r</code> lists applied and denied GPOs with the reason.', e: '<b>GPO scoping:</b> gpresult /h shows Denied (Security, WMI filter, Disabled link). SYSVOL access: <code>dir \\\\lab.local\\SYSVOL</code>. Event log Microsoft-Windows-GroupPolicy/Operational. Replication problems between DCs show different GPT versions (<code>repadmin /showrepl</code>).' },
      { s: '<b>Alma/Rocky: realm join says "Couldn\'t authenticate" or "no such realm".</b> Either the DNS server is not the domain controller, or the clock is off.', m: '<b>realm join failure.</b> "Cannot resolve" = the box does not use the DC for DNS; "Clock skew" = chrony not synced; "Insufficient permissions" = the account may not create computer objects in that OU. Check <code>realm discover</code> output first.', e: '<b>realmd/adcli:</b> run with <code>--verbose</code>; confirm SRV via host, time via <code>chronyc tracking</code>, and that the OU in --computer-ou exists. sssd not resolving users after join: <code>sssctl domain-status</code>, check <code>/var/log/sssd/sssd_lab.local.log</code>, and that the host uses the DC for DNS.' }
    ]
  }
];

if (typeof module !== 'undefined') module.exports = { SITE: SITE, LESSONS: LESSONS, GROUPS: GROUPS, OSES: OSES };
